{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CwneqDbwLxPLfUFV3fta81","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# BASKETBLAM\n2\t\n3\t**Over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball. No fouls. No mercy. All BLAM.**\n4\t\n5\tThis is the build plan for the demo TASK.md describes. It is written for the developers (people or AI models) who will\n6\tbuild the game from TASK.md and this file alone, without the planner. Read TASK.md first, then this file once, top to\n7\tbottom; after that use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision, not a menu: where a number is given, start\n8\twith it and tune it with the dev sliders (§14). Where this file and TASK.md disagree, TASK.md wins.\n9\t\n10\t---\n11\t\n12\t## 0. At a glance\n13\t\n14\t| | |\n15\t|---|---|\n16\t| Title | **BASKETBLAM** (on screen); `Basketblam` in file names |\n17\t| Genre | Arcade sports: 2-on-2 basketball in the over-the-top arcade style, with a short story ladder |\n18\t| Perspective | Side-on **broadcast camera**: a custom orthographic view, pitch 30°, the court running left to right, panning with the ball; a dunk cam that zooms; a \"High\" camera option |\n19\t| Players | 1–4 on one machine (gamepads and a shared keyboard); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n20\t| Modes | Blam Tour (story, 4 games), Exhibition (any teams and courts, 1–4 players), Blam Camp (interactive tutorial), Hall of Blam (records), How to Play, Options, About/Credits; attract mode; dev mode; AI Hub \"Showdown\" |\n21\t| Content | 5 courts, each with its own twist; 6 teams (4 street crews, a robot boss team, a secret team), 18 players and 5 story characters; 12 dunks; 6 power-ups; 10 tutorial lessons; 15 story beats; ~200 announcer lines; 12 songs and 8 stings; about 55 sound effects; 21 voice profiles |\n22\t| Tech | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, our modified copy), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio; Node 24 + Playwright 1.63 for tests |\n23\t| Deliverable | one self-contained HTML file in the root, plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n24\t\n25\t**The core loop, in one sentence:** get the ball, beat your defender with turbo, spin moves and passes, and finish with a\n26\tdunk that leaves the atmosphere or a jumper released at the top of the jump; on defense swipe, swat and shove (it's\n27\tlegal); score three straight and you go **SUPERNOVA**.\n28\t\n29\t**Pillars** (use them to settle any question this plan doesn't):\n30\t1. **Every possession can end in spectacle.** Dunks, blocks, shoves and steals are big, readable and loud.\n31\t2. **Simple buttons, real timing.** Four main buttons; skill lives in release timing, turbo management, alley-oop\n32\t   timing and steal angles.\n33\t3. **Personality everywhere.** Two hype announcers, trash talk, courts with twists, a crowd that reacts.\n34\t4. **Couch first, 10 feet away.** Up to four on one screen, a TV-readable HUD, controller-first menus.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 1. Title, names and folders\n39\t\n40\t- **Title: BASKETBLAM.** Picked as TASK.md asks: fifty riffs on \"basketball\" were brainstormed and one was chosen with a\n41\t  real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 18). Do not re-pick it.\n42\t- **SOURCE token** in file names: `Basketball`.\n43\t- **Names for this TASK.md** (copy them exactly):\n44\t  - dev folder: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/` (in the root, beside TASK.md)\n45\t  - deliverable: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822.html` (in the root)\n46\t  - exit interview: `exit-interview.json` (in the root, beside the HTML)\n47\t\n48\t  If your TASK.md names another model or timestamp, use your TASK.md's names with the same `Basketblam` and\n49\t  `Basketball` tokens.\n50\t- **Dev folder layout:**\n51\t\n52\t```\n53\tBasketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/\n54\t  handoff/            pristine copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (docs, tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, asset tools)\n55\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js our modified engine (every change marked /* BASKETBLAM: ... */)\n56\t  vendor/aihub-session.js       AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n57\t  vendor/hub-harness/           harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n58\t  src/*.js            the game, as numbered fragments (§4.2)\n59\t  tools/build.mjs     concatenates src into build/game.js, writes build/index.html and the root deliverable\n60\t  tests/run.mjs       the one test command (§19); tests/*.test.mjs; tests/helpers.mjs; tests/fakehub.mjs\n61\t  build/              generated: game.js, index.html (dev page), hub/ (served for harness tests)\n62\t  check-output/       screenshots and filmstrips for review (generated)\n63\t  package.json        { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n64\t```\n65\t\n66\t---\n67\t\n68\t## 2. Genre, perspective and look\n69\t\n70\t**Genre: arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball.** The over-the-top arcade style is defined by two-on-two, turbo, legal\n71\tcontact, impossible dunks, a hot-streak power and loud personality. Those live best in short matches; a four-game story\n72\tladder (the Tour) gives the demo an arc and story beats; Exhibition, local multiplayer and online show the rest.\n73\t\n74\t**Perspective: a side-on broadcast camera.** `const BROADCAST = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 30, 1, 1)`: yaw\n75\t0, pitch 30°, one screen pixel per world unit. The court's length runs left to right (world x), the camera sits beyond\n76\tthe near sideline looking across it, and pans horizontally with the ball. Why: the race up and down the court reads\n77\tleft/right, dunks and blocks read in height at a low pitch (z is drawn at cos 30° = 0.87, the court's depth at\n78\tsin 30° = 0.5), and below pitch 40 the engine draws rigs from the view itself, so jump poses read. The engine's\n79\t`'threequarter'` view (pitch 55) is offered as Settings → Camera → High; every custom draw uses `r.w()` projection so\n80\tboth views work (yaw is 0 in both, so the depth rules in §9 hold in both).\n81\t\n82\t**Look: the engine's native HD style, pushed to \"90s arcade broadcast\".** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; HD\n83\tHumanoids at size 1.6–1.95 (players 48–56 world units tall, about 42–48 px on screen); textured court floors painted by\n84\t`floorTex`; an `E.Backdrop` plus hand-drawn silhouettes behind every court; 3–8 props per screen; canvas lighting on\n85\tevery court; `E.font.title` logos and comic banners (\"BLAM!\", \"REJECTED!\"); particles, hit-stop, shake and flashes on\n86\tevery impact; broadcast graphics (a score bug, lower-third captions with live portraits). Saturated team colors, neon\n87\tat the night courts, chunky outlines. Compare screenshots with PS1-era 2D, not NES.\n88\t\n89\t---\n90\t\n91\t## 3. Tech stack and setup\n92\t\n93\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** (planned against repo commit `4390a985`, 2026-11-03), the full readable engine from\n94\t  `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` (it already contains every module, including `arcade`). We change it where the game needs\n95\t  (§5). If the repository has moved on, read the newer handoff `README.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` head for breaking changes.\n96\t- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`lib/hub/session.js`, ai-hub commit `f8835e48`), pasted\n97\t  unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`.\n98\t- **Code:** plain JavaScript in one IIFE (`const E = My3D2dge` first), no frameworks, no runtime network requests, no\n99\t  CDN at runtime (the hub forbids network traffic outside the hub; the bundle must be self-contained).\n100\t- **Tests:** Node 24 (`node:vm` headless runs), Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium (the handoff's pin) for browser checks,\n101\t  `python3 -m http.server` for the hub harness.\n102\t\n103\t**Setup (from the root folder):**\n104\t\n105\t```sh\n106\tDEV=Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822\n107\tmkdir -p $DEV && cd $DEV\n108\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .engine-src\n109\tgit -C .engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff && cp -r .engine-src/dist/handoff handoff && rm -rf .engine-src\n110\tmkdir -p engine vendor/hub-harness && cp handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js\n111\tB=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub\n112\tcurl -fsSL $B/session.js -o vendor/aihub-session.js\n113\tfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do curl -fsSL $B/$f -o vendor/hub-harness/$f; done\n114\tnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm pkg set type=module && npm install -D playwright@1.63.0\n115\tnpx playwright install chromium     # skip if ~/.cache/ms-playwright already has a matching chromium\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\t**Read before coding** (in `handoff/`): `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `kits/sports.js` (whole: our starting point for\n119\tthe ball, passes, `E.arc` lobs, formations, `allowJoin`, the proof and the bot), `game/test.mjs` and `kits/test.mjs`\n120\t(headless testing), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before writing any shape), and grep `API-INDEX.md` for names.\n121\t`vendor/hub-harness/README.md` is the hub protocol: read it whole before §15's milestone.\n122\t\n123\t**Environment rules** (from TASK.md, made concrete):\n124\t- Read only what you need; other agents share the parent directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n125\t- Prefer `file://` pages and the engine's headless tools. The hub harness needs a server: bind\n126\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (the OS picks the port; parse it from the server's first line), fetch\n127\t  `/game.html` and check its `<title>` is Basketblam's before trusting a result, and stop only that server, by its PID.\n128\t  Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n129\t- /tmp was 99% full on this machine while this plan was written. If npm, git or Playwright fail with `ENOSPC`, export\n130\t  `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` (inside the dev folder) and delete that folder when done.\n131\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\n132\t\n133\t---\n134\t\n135\t## 4. Architecture\n136\t\n137\t### 4.1 Shape of the program\n138\t\n139\tOne `E.Game`, a set of scenes, and game systems that are plain objects working on one match state. The match\n140\tsimulation never reads devices directly: every player slot reads a **command** each step from an **input source**\n141\t(local device, CPU brain, network, attract director). That one rule gives local multiplayer, AI teammates, the attract\n142\tmode, the dev mode's autopilot, the test bots and online play for the same code.\n143\t\n144\t```js\n145\tconst HUB = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active);               // true from the first line inside a hub session\n146\tconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: BROADCAST, views: ['broadcast', 'threequarter'],\n147\t  bg: '#05040a', id: 'basketblam', input: KEYS, autoPause: !HUB,\n148\t  menu: HUB ? false : { items: pauseItems, settings: GAME_SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n149\t```\n150\t\n151\t`pauseItems` is a function returning the list for the current context (the Shell evaluates `menu.items` when the pause\n152\tscreen opens and skips falsy entries; confirm in `Shell` around \"`name === 'pause'`\" and patch the engine if it does\n153\tnot). Items: `How to Play`, `Restart Game` (matches only), `Skip Cutscene` (story only), `Dev Mode`.\n154\t\n155\t### 4.2 Modules (src fragments, concatenated in this order into one IIFE)\n156\t\n157\t| File | Holds |\n158\t|---|---|\n159\t| `00-core.js` | `E` alias, constants (§6.1), `HUB` flag, the `game`, input preset `KEYS`, settings specs, the shared state `S`, the event bus, RNG streams, registries (`def`, `get`, `all`) |\n160\t| `01-tuning.js` | `TUNING` defaults, live values and slider metadata (Appendix A); `tune(key)` reads; `resetTuning()` |\n161\t| `02-data-teams.js` | teams, players (ratings, rig specs, voices, taunts, celebrations), NPCs |\n162\t| `03-data-courts.js` | courts (palette, floor style, backdrop, props, crowd, lights, music, twist), arena layout |\n163\t| `04-data-story.js` | the Tour, story beats (scripts), matchup trash talk, halftime gags, sponsor cards, credits and About text |\n164\t| `05-data-lines.js` | announcer line banks by event |\n165\t| `06-data-moves.js` | animation clips, dunk catalog, taunts, power-ups, cheat codes |\n166\t| `10-audio-music.js` | songs, stings, dynamic layers, crowd ambience |\n167\t| `11-audio-sfx.js` | sound definitions (`audio.define`), material-aware dribble, sound policy |\n168\t| `12-audio-voice.js` | the gibberish voice synth (§12.3) and its Dialog/caption hookup |\n169\t| `20-court-build.js` | arena TileMap, floor painting (lines, logos), stands, collision bounds, shapes for props |\n170\t| `21-court-draw.js` | hoops (stanchion, cheated backboard, rim halves, net simulation, shatter), crowd, backdrop silhouettes, lights |\n171\t| `22-twists.js` | Jackpot Spot, wind, oil slicks, eruptions and steam vents, Dome pyro, boss mechanics |\n172\t| `30-ball.js` | ball states, flight, floor/rim/backboard collisions, scoring detection, net contact, trails |\n173\t| `31-player.js` | player entity: body, ratings, state machine, turbo, juice, power-up effects |\n174\t| `32-anim.js` | rig factory (dressing), pose layer over `solvePose`, clips, dribble sync, dunk choreography |\n175\t| `33-actions.js` | shot model, passes and alley-oops, steals, shoves, spin moves, blocks, goaltending, rebounds |\n176\t| `34-ai.js` | CPU brains (offense, defense, rebounding, teammate commands), difficulty, comeback |\n177\t| `35-match.js` | match flow (tip-off, quarters, clocks, possession, inbounds, overtime), Supernova, shatter pause, power-up spawner, stats, style points |\n178\t| `36-fx.js` | camera director, dunk cam, banners, particle recipes, rumble, comfort-setting policy |\n179\t| `40-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot widgets (focus, cards, buttons, sliders, toggles, pips), glyph icons, prompt lines, mouse hover/click |\n180\t| `41-hud.js` | score bug, player plates, indicators, captions, warnings |\n181\t| `42-screens.js` | title, main menu, join, team/lineup/court select, matchup card, quarter breaks, results, Hall of Blam, How to Play, About/Credits, touch-only notice |\n182\t| `43-tour.js` | tour map, cutscene runner, tour progress and saves, ending, unlocks |\n183\t| `44-camp.js` | Blam Camp lessons |\n184\t| `45-attract.js` | idle detection and the attract director |\n185\t| `46-dev.js` | dev panel, overlays, scene jumps, cheats |\n186\t| `50-hub.js` | AI Hub integration (card, host, client, server, leave, time-up, end) |\n187\t| `90-start.js` | scene table, boot steps, deep links, `game.debug`, `window.__bb`, `game.start` |\n188\t\n189\tRules: content never edits systems (systems read registries); systems talk through the event bus; anything a test or\n190\tthe dev panel needs is reachable from `window.__bb`.\n191\t\n192\t### 4.3 One step (1/120 s) of a match\n193\t\n194\t1. **Commands:** for each of the 4 player slots, its input source writes a `Cmd` (below).\n195\t2. **Players:** state machines consume commands; locomotion; actions start (shots, passes, steals, shoves, spins,\n196\t   jumps); bodies move (`E.Body.update` against the arena map); rigs update (§7).\n197\t3. **Ball:** attached (held/dribbled) or simulated (substeps near rims), collisions, catches, scoring.\n198\t4. **Match:** clocks, possession, dead balls, inbounds, quarter ends, Supernova bookkeeping, spawner, twists.\n199\t5. **Events** emitted during 1–4 are delivered (announcer, audio, crowd, stats, fx, attract director, net).\n200\t6. **Camera director** sets focus, zoom and time scale.\n201\t\n202\t`Cmd` = `{ move: [x, y], shoot: { down, pressed, released }, pass: {...}, turbo: { down }, shove: {...}, oop: {...},\n203\ttaunt: { pressed } }`. Sources: `LocalSource(input)` (from `game.players[i]`), `CpuSource(brain)`, `NetSource(seat)`\n204\t(§15), `ScriptSource(steps)` (tests, attract). Swapping a slot's source is one assignment (dev autopilot, a player\n205\tleaving a hub round, a player joining Exhibition).\n206\t\n207\t### 4.4 State\n208\t\n209\t`S` holds: `scene`, `mode` (`'exhibition' | 'tour' | 'camp' | 'attract' | 'hub'`), `court`, `teams[2]` (team id,\n210\tlineup, bench), `players[4]` (slot 0–1 HOME, 2–3 AWAY), `ball`, `match` (phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score,\n211\tpossession, alternating arrow, streaks, stats, styleScore), `twist` state, `powerups`, `cheats`, `rng`, `fxRng`,\n212\t`events` (ring buffer of the last 64 for debug and the net), `settings` mirror, `progress` (tour, unlocks, records).\n213\t`serializeMatch()` and `applySnapshot()` (§15) live beside it from the first milestone, with a test.\n214\t\n215\t### 4.5 Events\n216\t\n217\t`emit(type, data)` / `on(type, fn)`. Types: `tipoff, possession, inbound, dribble, pass, catch, intercept, shotStart,\n218\trelease, rim, backboard, basket, miss, airball, block, goaltend, steal, fumble, shove, knockdown, spin, anklesBroken,\n219\trebound, putback, dunkStart, dunk, posterized, alleyoop, supernovaOn, supernovaOff, shatter, sponsorBreak, powerupSpawn,\n220\tpowerupPick, powerupEnd, jackpot, twist (with kind), shotClockViolation, quarterEnd, halftime, overtime, gameEnd,\n221\ttaunt, cheat`. Each carries ids, positions and context (`points`, `dunkId`, `dist`, `contested`, `perfect`...).\n222\t\n223\t### 4.6 Randomness and determinism\n224\t\n225\t- `S.rng = E.rng(seed)`: every gameplay decision (shot outcomes, AI choices, steals, spawns, twist timing).\n226\t- `S.fxRng`: cosmetic choices (crowd, which line the announcer says). Particles may use `Math.random`.\n227\t- Tests seed `Math.random` too (as the kits' `proof()` does) so the rigs and particles repeat.\n228\t- Same seed + same commands = same match. A `proof(n)` function hashes the state after n scripted steps (copy the\n229\t  shell's `proof()` from `kits/sports.js`).\n230\t\n231\t### 4.7 Tuning\n232\t\n233\t`TUNING` is the single home of the important numbers (Appendix A): `{ key: { v, def, min, max, step, group, label,\n234\thelp } }`. Systems read `tune('runSpeed')` every time (never cache), so a dev slider changes play at once.\n235\t`resetTuning()` restores `def`. Dev mode can save the current values to `E.store('bb:tuning')` and copy them as JSON.\n236\t\n237\t### 4.8 Persistence\n238\t\n239\t`E.store` keys: `bb:progress` (`{ tour: { team, stop, score, cheatsUsed, beatsSeen }, unlocked: { bots, allstars,\n240\tbigHeads }, camp: [done lesson ids], records: { mostPoints, bestDunk, toursWon } }`), `bb:tuning`. The Tour also uses\n241\t`game.saves` autosave (`write`/`read`, `autosave()` after each stop, `latest()` for CONTINUE TOUR). Hall of Blam\n242\ttables use `E.Arcade` (`id: 'basketblam'`, mode `'tour'`). In hub sessions the client gives the game an empty\n243\tin-memory storage: nothing to do.\n244\t\n245\t### 4.9 Deep links and the debug handle\n246\t\n247\tDeep links (the page's `#hash`, read in `90-start.js`): `#title`, `#attract`, `#exhibition/<court>/<home>-<away>`,\n248\t`#tour/<stop 1-4>`, `#story/<beatId>`, `#camp/<lessonId>`, `#results`, `#credits`, `#about`, `#dev` (an exhibition with\n249\tthe dev panel open), `#animlab`, `#shootaround`. `#animlab` (every clip on a rig, slow motion, scrub) and\n250\t`#shootaround` (rim physics bench) are dev tools, also reachable from the dev panel's SCENES tab.\n251\t\n252\t`game.debug = { snapshot(), teleport(slot, x, y), give(slot), setScore(a, b), setClock(s), setQuarter(q), force(name,\n253\targs), go(scene, data), spawnPowerup(id, x, y), cheat(name, on), tune(key, v) }`. `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode,\n254\tcourt, phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score, possession, ball: { x, y, z, state, owner }, players: [{ slot, id, team,\n255\tx, y, z, state, turbo, juice, nova, powerup, source }], lastEvents: [10 types], ui: { screen, focus }, attract:\n256\t{ segment }, net: { role, seat, rtt } }`. `window.__bb = { S, game, TUNING, emit, on, debug: game.debug }`.\n257\t\n258\t---\n259\t\n260\t## 5. Engine changes (planned)\n261\t\n262\tMake each in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, mark it `/* BASKETBLAM: why */`, and list it on the About screen and in the exit\n263\tinterview's engine feedback.\n264\t\n265\t1. **Basketball shorts on `Humanoid`:** option `shorts: { len: .55, sock: '#ffffff', sockLen: .35 }`. In the HD leg\n266\t   drawing (`limb(hip, knee, ..., C.pants)`, `limb(knee, ank, ...)`, about line 4388) draw the thigh in `C.pants` only\n267\t   down to `len` of its length (shorts with a hem in `C.trim`), the rest of the thigh and the shin in `C.skin`, and the\n268\t   last `sockLen` of the shin in `sock`; the same in the classic path (about line 4576).\n269\t2. **Hair styles** `'afro'` (a big shaded ball around the back and top of the head), `'flattop'` (a tall flat box),\n270\t   `'mohawk'` (a ridge of spikes front to back) in the HD head drawing beside `'spiky'` (about line 4493), and in\n271\t   `drawPortrait`.\n272\t3. **`Dialog.voice` may be a function** `(letter, index, pageText)` called for each letter typed (about line 8956),\n273\t   so the gibberish voice speaks the line as it types.\n274\t4. **Public audio buses:** `audio.sfxBus` and `audio.musicBus` getters for `_sfxBus` / `_musBus`, so the voice and crowd\n275\t   nodes follow the volume settings.\n276\t5. Any engine bug found while building: fix it in the engine (not around it), with a test, and log it for the\n277\t   exit interview.\n278\t\n279\tBig heads need no engine change: `rig.o.headR` is read when drawing, so the cheat multiplies it (keep the original to\n280\trestore it).\n281\t\n282\t---\n283\t\n284\t## 6. Court, rules and core mechanics\n285\t\n286\t### 6.1 Court geometry (world units; one tile = 16; z up)\n287\t\n288\tArena map: 48 × 28 tiles (768 × 448). Court-local coordinates `(cx, cy)`: x 0..640 baseline to baseline, y 0..288 far\n289\tsideline to near sideline. World = court-local + `(64, 112)`. Constants (court-local):\n290\t\n291\t| Thing | Value |\n292\t|---|---|\n293\t| Court | 640 × 288; centre (320, 144) |\n294\t| Rim centres | (52, 144) and (588, 144); z 80; inner radius 9; tube radius 1.2 |\n295\t| Backboards (collision) | planes x = 40 and x = 600, y 120..168, z 72..100 |\n296\t| Ball | radius 4.5 |\n297\t| Three-point line | arc radius 136 from the rim centre; straight corner lines at y = 20 and y = 268 from the baseline to x = 108 (mirrored at the other end) |\n298\t| Key (paint) | y 96..192, baseline to x = 152 (free-throw line); free-throw circle r 48 |\n299\t| Centre circle | r 40; half-court line x = 320 |\n300\t| Out of bounds | none: invisible walls 24 units outside the lines stop players; the ball bounces off them (the crowd) |\n301\t| Players | 48–56 tall; body radius 6; standing reach about z 59 (size 1.7) |\n302\t\n303\tNorth of the court (screen top): an apron (16) and stands of 4 tiers (heights 12/24/36/48) with the crowd. West and\n304\teast: 64 of apron with the stanchion bases, benches, props. South (camera side): 48 of apron with low props (scorer's\n305\ttable, cameras) drawn with `cut` so they never hide play. One player is controlled by each human; there is no switching\n306\tof players (the genre's rule).\n307\t\n308\t### 6.2 Controls and actions\n309\t\n310\t| Action | Pad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse | With the ball | Without the ball |\n311\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n312\t| move | left stick, d-pad | WASD (arrows for a lone player too) | – | run / dribble | run, defend |\n313\t| `shoot` | □ Square (`Pad2`) | Space, J | left button | hold: jump; release at the top to shoot; in dunk range with turbo: dunk | jump to block or rebound; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"shoot it!\" |\n314\t| `pass` | ✕ Cross (`Pad0`) | E, K | right button | pass to your teammate (led) | steal swipe; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"pass it to me!\" |\n315\t| `turbo` | R2 (`Pad7`), R1 (`Pad5`) | Shift (left or right) | – | hold: faster, higher, dunks | hold: faster, higher |\n316\t| `shove` | ○ Circle (`Pad1`) | Q, L | – | SPIN MOVE | SHOVE |\n317\t| `oop` | △ Triangle (`Pad3`) | F, I | – | alley-oop lob to your teammate | call for the ball; CPU teammate with the ball throws you an oop if you're in range |\n318\t| `taunt` | L1 (`Pad4`) | R, U | – | – | taunt (dead balls or without the ball; off in hub sessions) |\n319\t| `pause` / menus | Options, ✕/○ | Esc/P, Enter/Esc | click, right-click back | | |\n320\t\n321\tPreset: `const KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, { shoot: ['Space', 'KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad2'], pass: ['KeyE',\n322\t'KeyK', 'Mouse2', 'Pad0'], turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7', 'Pad5'], shove: ['KeyQ', 'KeyL', 'Pad1'], oop:\n323\t['KeyF', 'KeyI', 'Pad3'], taunt: ['KeyR', 'KeyU', 'Pad4'] })`, minus `attack, dash, skill, jump`. Names for the Controls\n324\tscreen: `{ shoot: 'Shoot / Block', pass: 'Pass / Steal', turbo: 'Turbo', shove: 'Shove / Spin', oop: 'Alley-oop / Call',\n325\ttaunt: 'Taunt' }`. Two players on one keyboard get the engine's halves (`E.Input.halfKeys`): check what it assigns and\n326\tshow it on the join screen. Combo shortcut kept for genre veterans: turbo + pass without the ball also shoves.\n327\t\n328\t### 6.3 Match flow and rules (\"What you can and can't do\")\n329\t\n330\t- **2 on 2, full court, 4 quarters.** Quarter length: Exhibition from Settings (1:00 / 1:30 / 2:00 / 3:00, default\n331\t  2:00); Tour 1:30; hub 2 halves of 1:30. The game clock runs only while the ball is live; it stops on made baskets\n332\t  until the inbound is caught, and during breaks.\n333\t- **Tip-off** starts the game and each overtime: the two jumpers (highest SWAT on each side) at centre; REF ROSCOE\n334\t  tosses the ball (apex z 120 at 0.9 s); press shoot to jump; the jump whose hands peak nearest the ball's apex wins\n335\t  (CPU timing error by difficulty) and taps it to the teammate. Quarters 2–4 begin with an inbound at half court by the\n336\t  alternating arrow (the team that lost the tip gets Q2 and Q3).\n337\t- **Scoring:** 2 points; 3 from beyond the arc (by the shooter's takeoff point); dunks 2; Jackpot Spot +2; Gold Ball\n338\t  doubles. A shot released before the buzzer counts.\n339\t- **No fouls.** Shoving, steals and body contact are legal. No traveling, no backcourt, no three seconds, no out of\n340\t  bounds (the walls keep everything in).\n341\t- **Shot clock 20 s;** resets on a change of possession and when a shot touches the rim. Expiry: buzzer, turnover,\n342\t  inbound by the other team from the nearest sideline.\n343\t- **Goaltending:** touching the ball while it is coming down (vz < 0) within 18 units of the rim centre and above the\n344\t  rim counts the basket for the shooter (\"GOALTEND!\"). Supernova players and BLAMBOTS ignore this rule.\n345\t- **After a made basket** the other team inbounds from under the basket it defends: the inbounder passes in after 0.8 s\n346\t  (or when its human presses pass). Defenders may not stand within 24 units of the inbounder; the inbound pass can be\n347\t  intercepted only by a defender standing in its lane.\n348\t- **Tie after Q4:** overtime of 1:00, again if tied; after two overtimes, SUDDEN BLAM: the next basket wins (so every\n349\t  game ends, which the tests rely on).\n350\t- **Breaks:** after Q1 and Q3 a short break card (6 s, subs allowed); after Q2 the Halftime Report (stats, a gag, subs).\n351\t\n352\t### 6.4 Ratings\n353\t\n354\tEach player has eight ratings, 1–10, shown as pips with icons (never raw numbers on the 10-foot screens):\n355\t\n356\t| Rating | Means | Effect (r = rating) |\n357\t|---|---|---|\n358\t| ZOOM | speed | run speed × (0.86 + 0.028 r) |\n359\t| SPLASH | two-point jumpers, layups | make chance × (0.78 + 0.045 r) |\n360\t| DEEP | three-pointers | make chance × (0.70 + 0.06 r) |\n361\t| SLAM | dunking | dunk range × (0.75 + 0.05 r); unlocks dunks (§10.3); resists blocks |\n362\t| DIME | passing, handles | pass speed 180 + 12 r; interception window; ball security vs steals; alley-oop range |\n363\t| SWIPE | steals | steal chance × (0.55 + 0.09 r); steal reach 14 + 0.6 r |\n364\t| SWAT | blocks, rebounds | jump speed 250 + 10 r; block reach 10 + 0.8 r; block chance |\n365\t| BUMP | power | shove force 140 + 14 r; knockdown time; fumble chance; shove resistance; box-outs |\n366\t\n367\tEvery effect is multiplied by the juice factor (§6.14) and by power-ups and Supernova where they apply.\n368\t\n369\t### 6.5 Movement and turbo\n370\t\n371\t- Velocity approaches `stick × runSpeed × ZOOM factor` at `accel` (900 u/s²). Facing turns toward the movement; on\n372\t  defense with the opponent holding the ball, the defender faces the ball handler and shuffles (defensive stance).\n373\t- Bodies are `E.Body` (radius 6, gravity `playerGravity` 900) on the arena TileMap; players push each other apart\n374\t  (separation, no damage); `E.SpatialHash` is unnecessary at four players.\n375\t- **Turbo** (0..1 per player): held and moving drains `turboDrain` 0.30/s; not held refills `turboRefill` 0.20/s after\n376\t  `turboRefillDelay` 0.35 s; speed × `turboMult` 1.5, jumps × 1.15; required to start a dunk (≥ 0.05); a shove costs\n377\t  0.25, a spin move 0.2. Supernova and Rocket Kicks change it (§6.12, §6.15).\n378\t- A sharp change of direction at speed squeaks the sneakers and kicks dust.\n379\t\n380\t### 6.6 The ball\n381\t\n382\tStates: `held` (by its owner's hands), `dribble` (owner; position from the dribble cycle, §7), `pass` (flying to a\n383\ttarget), `shot` (flying with its shot record: shooter, points, decided outcome, release time, perfect flag), `loose`\n384\t(free physics), `dead` (scripted: after a score, during breaks), `inbound`.\n385\t\n386\t- Gravity `ballGravity` 760; floor bounce restitution `ballBounce` 0.62 with friction; rolling decays.\n387\t- **Rim:** a torus (ring radius 9, tube 1.2). Closest point Q on the ring circle to the ball's centre; if\n388\t  `|ball − Q| < 4.5 + 1.2` reflect the velocity along the normal with `rimBounce` 0.45 and a little friction; the rim\n389\t  \"rattles\" (visual bend + clank). Substep the ball 4× (1/480 s) within 40 units of a rim; never let it tunnel.\n390\t- **Backboard:** the plane x = 40 (or 600) within its y/z bounds; restitution 0.55; a thud.\n391\t- **Arena walls:** 24 outside the lines; restitution 0.5.\n392\t- **Basket:** the ball's centre crosses z = 80 downward within 6.75 of the rim centre: score once (a `scored` flag\n393\t  holds until it falls below z 60); the net slows it (vz × 0.6) as it passes.\n394\t- Players do not collide with the ball except to catch, steal, block, tip or goaltend it.\n395\t\n396\t### 6.7 Shooting\n397\t\n398\tPressing shoot with the ball (out of dunk range, or without turbo) starts a **jump shot**: gather 0.08 s, jump\n399\t(`jumpShotVz` 250 + SWAT bonus), the ball rises above the forehead. Releasing the button releases the ball; holding past\n400\tthe top releases it automatically 0.25 s after the top (a late release). The **release meter** (a thin arc around the\n401\tshooter, Settings → Shot meter, on by default) fills toward the top of the jump; the PERFECT window is `perfectWindow`\n402\t0.10 s wide, centred on the top.\n403\t\n404\tThe outcome is decided at release, then flown physically:\n405\t\n406\t```\n407\tp = base(dist) × ratingFactor × release × contest × shotSkill × (3PT ? threeSkill : 1) × juice + bonuses\n408\tbase(dist): dist ≤ 40: 0.85 · 40–80: 0.85→0.70 · 80–136: 0.70→0.45 · 136–200: 0.38→0.30 · 200–320: 0.30→0.08 · beyond: 0.03\n409\tratingFactor: SPLASH (2PT) or DEEP (3PT) factor (§6.4)\n410\trelease: PERFECT × (1 + perfectBonus 0.15) · inside 2× the window × 1 · early or late × (1 − badReleasePenalty 0.30)\n411\tcontest: nearest defender between shooter and rim within 30 and airborne × (1 − contestPenalty 0.40); within 30 on the floor × 0.80; within 50 × 0.92\n412\tbonuses (added): Supernova + novaShotBonus 0.25 · Laser Sight + 0.30 · comeback assist (§8, Comeback assist)\n413\tmodifiers: turbo held × 1.05 · drifting sideways fast × 0.90 · wind (the Pier) × (1 − 0.35 × |wind| × dist / 320)\n414\tclamp to [0.02, 0.97]\n415\t```\n416\t\n417\t`made = S.rng() < p`. The flight is solved with `E.arc(release, target, ballGravity, { apex })`, apex = max(release z,\n418\trim z) + clamp(18 + dist × 0.10, 18, 60) × `shotArc`. Made: the target is the rim centre 2 below the rim plane, entering\n419\ton the way down (a swish); 1 in 4 makes target a point 3 off the centre and are helped in if they touch the rim (a\n420\t\"rattle in\": nudge the velocity toward the centre). Missed: the target is a point on the front rim, back rim, side rim or\n421\tthe backboard, chosen by the error, so the ball bounces off naturally; if physics drops a decided miss in anyway (rare)\n422\tit counts (it's a real basket). A miss by more than 30 units from the rim is an **airball**. Wind adds a constant\n423\thorizontal acceleration during flight: solve the launch with it included so the ball curves and still reaches the\n424\tdecided target. Tests hold made rate to the decided rate within 3% (§19).\n425\t\n426\t### 6.8 Layups, dunks, alley-oops, putbacks\n427\t\n428\t- **Layup:** shoot pressed within 40 units of the rim without turbo, or with turbo when the player can't dunk: a short\n429\t  one-handed rise and scoop; make chance from the 0..40 band (high); blockable.\n430\t- **Dunk:** shoot pressed with turbo held inside the dunk range: `dunkRange` 150 × SLAM factor (Supernova + 40, Moon\n431\t  Shoes to the three-point line + 20, steam vent: anywhere in that half). The dunk is chosen from the catalog (§10.3) by\n432\t  distance, approach angle, SLAM rating and context (weighted by `S.rng`). Flight: from takeoff to the slam point (the\n433\t  feet where the dunking hand reaches 6 above the rim; about feet z 28 at size 1.7) with `E.arc(…, dunkGravity 520,\n434\t  { apex: slamFeetZ + apexAbove × dunkApex })`, time × `dunkHang`. The ball rides the hands (§7); at the slam the ball is\n435\t  forced down through the hoop (a made basket), the rim bends and snaps back, and the dunker either drops or hangs.\n436\t  A dunk scores unless it is **blocked** before the slam: a defender in the air whose block hands meet the ball, with\n437\t  chance `clamp(0.25 + 0.06 × (SWAT_def − SLAM_off) + timing, 0.05, 0.75) × blockChance`. Players with SLAM ≤ 3 miss\n438\t  4% of dunks off the back rim (comedy).\n439\t- **Alley-oop:** the ball handler presses `oop` while the teammate is within the oop range (dunk range × 1.15 + DIME\n440\t  × 4) of the rim and not covered tightly: the teammate leaps (dunk flight to the rim) and the passer lobs to meet the\n441\t  hands at 70% of the flight (`E.arc` with the flight's time). A defender in the lob's path can intercept (steal), one\n442\t  in the flight's path can block. Out of range, `oop` throws a plain lob to the teammate. A human receiver whose CPU\n443\t  teammate has the ball presses `oop` to ask for it.\n444\t- **Putback:** catching an offensive rebound in the air within 40 units of the rim, then pressing shoot (or holding\n445\t  turbo) slams it at once (\"PUTBACK!\").\n446\t- **Posterized:** a dunk slammed within 24 units of a defender in front of the rim knocks that defender down with dizzy\n447\t  stars (\"POSTERIZED!\").\n448\t\n449\t### 6.9 Passing\n450\t\n451\t`pass` with the ball: a chest pass at chest height along the floor plane to the teammate, led by the receiver's velocity\n452\t(`kits/sports.js`'s `pass()`), speed `180 + 12 × DIME` × 1.6; a bounce pass when a defender's hands are near the lane\n453\t(cosmetic: one floor bounce). The receiver catches when the ball passes within 12 of its hands. Interception: a defender\n454\twithin 10 of the ball's line, chance by SWIPE vs DIME (0.15 to 0.55). Commands to a CPU teammate holding the ball:\n455\t`pass` = pass to me, `shoot` = shoot now, `oop` = throw me an oop.\n456\t\n457\t### 6.10 Defense: steals, shoves, spin moves, blocks\n458\t\n459\t- **Steal** (`pass` on defense): a 0.2 s swipe, lunge 6, reach `stealReach` 18 (+ SWIPE). Chance per attempt:\n460\t  `stealChance 0.30 × SWIPE factor × (1.25 − 0.05 × DIME_handler) × angle × context`; angle × 1.4 from behind or the\n461\t  dribble-hand side, × 0.6 from the off-hand front; handler on turbo × 1.15; handler spinning or in a shot: 0. Success\n462\t  pops the ball loose toward the stealer (or straight to its hands within 8). Cooldown `stealCooldown` 0.6 s; a miss\n463\t  leaves a 0.2 s recovery.\n464\t- **Shove** (`shove` on defense, or turbo + pass): wind 0.06 s, a two-handed push with lunge 10, reach 22, ±60° arc;\n465\t  cost 0.25 turbo (needs ≥ 0.1), cooldown `shoveCooldown` 1.0 s. A hit knocks the target down for\n466\t  `knockdownTime 0.8 + 0.04 × (BUMP_att − BUMP_def)` (0.3..1.4 s), sliding back with `shoveForce` 220 × BUMP factor.\n467\t  A ball handler fumbles with chance `shoveFumble 0.6 + 0.04 × (BUMP_att − BUMP_def)` (0.2..0.95); a shooter in the air\n468\t  loses the shot with 0.7. Shoving costs the target juice.\n469\t- **Spin move** (`shove` with the ball): a 0.35 s 360° spin dribble (`rig.spin`) carrying the handler 30 forward;\n470\t  steals fail during it; a defender within 14 in front stumbles 0.4 s (\"ANKLES BROKEN!\"). Cost 0.2 turbo; cooldown 1.5 s.\n471\t- **Block** (`shoot` on defense): a jump (`250 + 10 × SWAT`, × 1.15 with turbo) with both arms up; during the jump a\n472\t  swat swing triggers when the ball comes within `blockReach` (14 + SWAT); against a shot in its first 0.25 s of flight,\n473\t  a layup or a dunk before the slam it succeeds with the block chance; a block sends the ball flying (toward half court\n474\t  if possible) and emits `block` (\"REJECTED!\"). Touching a descending shot is goaltending (§6.3).\n475\t\n476\t### 6.11 Rebounds and loose balls\n477\t\n478\tA missed shot that touches the rim or board is `loose`. Everyone can catch a loose ball within reach (horizontal 10,\n479\tvertical from the feet to the standing reach, plus the jump). `shoot` near a loose ball in the air jumps for it\n480\t(rebound). Box-out: a player with higher BUMP between an opponent and the ball holds position (the other is nudged\n481\taside). Loose balls on the floor are picked up by touching them.\n482\t\n483\t### 6.12 SUPERNOVA\n484\t\n485\t- **Ignites** when one player scores `novaBaskets` 3 baskets in a row for the team with no basket by the other team in\n486\t  between. The HUD shows each player's streak as three heat pips.\n487\t- **While on:** unlimited turbo, + `novaShotBonus` 0.25 to every shot, + 40 dunk range, + 20% steal chance, goaltending\n488\t  allowed, dunks shatter the glass with 35%, the ball leaves a comet trail of star-fire (`r.actor` ghosts on the player,\n489\t  glints and embers on the ball), the rim glows, the crowd chants, the music adds its \"nova\" layer.\n490\t- **Ends** when the other team scores, after `novaMaxBaskets` 4 more baskets, or after `novaTime` 40 s of game clock.\n491\t\n492\t### 6.13 Backboard shatter\n493\t\n494\tA dunk shatters the backboard with chance 0 normally, 3% for SLAM ≥ 9 turbo dunks, 35% in Supernova, 50% with Moon\n495\tShoes, 20% for the Volcano Launch, 100% for BLAM-QUAKE (all × `shatterChance`), at most twice a game. The basket counts;\n496\tglass shards (`particles.bits` in pale blue and white, glints) rain; hit-stop 0.15, flash, slow motion 0.3 for 0.5 s;\n497\tthen the **SPONSOR BREAK**: a 4-second fake commercial card (\"WE'LL BE RIGHT BACK AFTER THIS MESSAGE FROM GLOBO-BLAM\n498\tGLASS: NOW 40% LESS SHATTERY!\") with its jingle, while a cleaning bot rolls out and fits new glass; then the inbound.\n499\t\n500\t### 6.14 Juice and substitutions\n501\t\n502\tEach player has juice 0..1 (starts 1). It drops 0.06 per knockdown suffered, 0.01 per second of turbo, 0.004 per second\n503\ton court (× `fatigue`); a break restores 0.25 to players on court and 0.5 to the bench. The juice factor\n504\t`0.85 + 0.15 × juice` multiplies the ratings' effects. Each team carries 3 players: at each break, a human picks who\n505\tplays (the break screen shows juice bars); CPU teams bench the lowest juice. Hub rounds have no subs.\n506\t\n507\t### 6.15 Power-ups and the Jackpot Spot\n508\t\n509\tPower-ups appear in Exhibition (Settings → Power-ups, default on), on Tour stops 2–4 and the final, in attract mode and\n510\tin hub rounds. One on the floor at a time (two in attract), every `powerupInterval` 22 s (± 6), lasting 10 s, never on\n511\ta twist hazard; run over it to take it; its effect shows as an icon and a countdown on the player's plate.\n512\t\n513\t| Id | Name | Effect | Lasts |\n514\t|---|---|---|---|\n515\t| `rocket` | ROCKET KICKS | speed + 40%, turbo refills twice as fast | 15 s |\n516\t| `glue` | GLUE GLOVES | steals always succeed against players without Glue Gloves; you can't be stripped | 15 s |\n517\t| `laser` | LASER SIGHT | + 0.30 shot chance; the PERFECT window doubles | next 2 shots or 15 s |\n518\t| `moon` | MOON SHOES | dunk range to the three-point line + 20; jumps × 1.5; dunks shatter 50% | 15 s |\n519\t| `bomb` | BLAM BOMB | your next shove is a shockwave: knocks down every opponent within 60, always fumbles | one use, 20 s |\n520\t| `gold` | GOLD BALL | your team's next basket counts double | until used, 30 s |\n521\t\n522\t**Jackpot Spot** (the Skybox, and Exhibition with Settings → Jackpot Spot on): a glowing ring (radius 16) appears at a\n523\trandom place inside the three-point range of one basket for 20 s, then moves; a made jump shot taken with the feet in\n524\tit scores + 2 (\"JACKPOT!\").\n525\t\n526\t### 6.16 Stats and style points\n527\t\n528\tPer player: PTS, FGM/FGA, 3PM/3PA, DUNKS, REB, AST, STL, BLK, KNOCKDOWNS, SHATTERS, NOVAS. **Style points** feed the\n529\tTour score and the Hall of Blam: dunk 200 + the dunk's own bonus, three 150, alley-oop 250, block 100, steal 80,\n530\tputback 150, posterized 200, shatter 1000, Supernova 300, jackpot 250, ankles broken 100; a win adds 2000 + 50 per\n531\tpoint of margin. A game played with cheats is marked ★ and can't enter the Hall of Blam.\n532\t\n533\t---\n534\t\n535\t## 7. Animation\n536\t\n537\tThe base is the engine's own: Humanoid rigs posed by math each step (`rig.update`), its locomotion, `air`, `flip`,\n538\t`pose` (`cheer`, `hips`, `wave`, `guard`, `crouch`, `down`), `expr`, `hurt`, `land()`, `kick()` and attack specs. On\n539\ttop we add a **pose layer** for everything basketball: hands that hold and dribble the ball, shooting form, dunk\n540\tchoreography, hanging on the rim, celebrations and taunts.\n541\t\n542\t### 7.1 The pose layer\n543\t\n544\t```js\n545\tconst base = rig.solvePose;\n546\trig.solvePose = function () { base.call(this); poseLayer(this, this.bb); };   // bb: the player's anim state\n547\t```\n548\t\n549\t`poseLayer` reads the player's current clip and time, interpolates the clip's keys (eased), and for each hand with a\n550\ttarget solves the arm with `E.ik3(shoulder, target, armUpper × size, armLower × size, elbowHint)`, writing\n551\t`J.elbowR/handR` (and L). Targets are written `[forward, right, up]` in the rig's own frame at size 1 and multiplied by\n552\t`rig.o.size`; check the units against how `solvePose` builds `J` (read it once) before writing clips. A clip may also\n553\tset body tweaks (lean, a crouch amount, a hop) and a `pose` / `expr` it passes to `rig.update`. Spins rotate the\n554\t`facing` given to `rig.update` (2π over the move); flips use the rig's `flip: 0..1`. The ball attaches to the hand(s):\n555\tits world point is `rig.worldOffset(J.handR)` (or the mid-point of both hands) plus the rig's position, nudged 4.5 out\n556\tof the palm.\n557\t\n558\t### 7.2 The dribble (procedural, synced to the ball)\n559\t\n560\tA phase φ in 0..1 advances at 2.2 Hz standing, 3.0 running, 3.8 on turbo. The ball falls from the hand (about hip\n561\theight) to the floor (contact at φ = 0.5, the dribble sound for the court's floor) and back up, as two parabolas; the\n562\thand is in contact for φ in [0.85, 1] ∪ [0, 0.15], pushing down at φ ≈ 0.05, and hovers above the ball otherwise. The\n563\tball sits 5 forward and 6 to the dribble side (× size). **Crossover:** when the move direction turns more than 100° or a\n564\tdefender reaches the dribble side within 20, the next bounce lands on the other side (the ball crosses in front) and\n565\tthe hand switches. The off hand guards (forearm out toward the nearest defender).\n566\t\n567\t### 7.3 Clips (data in `06-data-moves.js`; targets at size 1)\n568\t\n569\t| Clip | Length | Hands and body |\n570\t|---|---|---|\n571\t| `hold` (triple threat) | loop | both hands at [4, ±1.5, 15], ball between; slight crouch |\n572\t| `gather` | .08 s | ball to the chest [4, 0, 20] |\n573\t| `jumpshot` | rise → release → follow | rise: right hand under the ball at [3, 1, 33], left at its side [2, −3, 30]; release (.1 s): right arm to [5, 1.5, 38]; the follow-through holds until landing |\n574\t| `layup` | rise | right hand scoops [5, 2, 16] → [6, 2, 40]; left guards [3, −3, 26] |\n575\t| `passChest` | .08 + .12 s | both hands [4, ±1.5, 19] → [11, ±1, 20] |\n576\t| `passBounce` | .08 + .12 s | both hands [4, ±1.5, 18] → [10, ±1, 10] |\n577\t| `passLob` (and oop lob, inbound) | .1 + .15 s | both hands [2, ±1.5, 22] → [6, ±1, 38] overhead |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0432b045-5008-4207-9914-efcb595d2dad","uuid":"0b84f742-03d8-4b5b-a7f1-8a504c1f4680","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:18:36.293Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-061830__x5a4f79/work/PLAN.md","content":"# BASKETBLAM\n\n**Over-the-top 2-on-2 arcade basketball. No fouls. No mercy. All BLAM.**\n\nThis is the build plan for the demo TASK.md describes. It is written for the developers (people or AI models) who will\nbuild the game from TASK.md and this file alone, without the planner. Read TASK.md first, then this file once, top to\nbottom; after that use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision, not a menu: where a number is given, start\nwith it and tune it with the dev sliders (§14). Where this file and TASK.md disagree, TASK.md wins.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Title | **BASKETBLAM** (on screen); `Basketblam` in file names |\n| Genre | Arcade sports: 2-on-2 basketball in the over-the-top arcade style, with a short story ladder |\n| Perspective | Side-on **broadcast camera**: a custom orthographic view, pitch 30°, the court running left to right, panning with the ball; a dunk cam that zooms; a \"High\" camera option |\n| Players | 1–4 on one machine (gamepads and a shared keyboard); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (shared world) |\n| Modes | Blam Tour (story, 4 games), Exhibition (any teams and courts, 1–4 players), Blam Camp (interactive tutorial), Hall of Blam (records), How to Play, Options, About/Credits; attract mode; dev mode; AI Hub \"Showdown\" |\n| Content | 5 courts, each with its own twist; 6 teams (4 street crews, a robot boss team, a secret team), 18 players and 5 story characters; 12 dunks; 6 power-ups; 10 tutorial lessons; 15 story beats; ~200 announcer lines; 12 songs and 8 stings; about 55 sound effects; 21 voice profiles |\n| Tech | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, our modified copy), AI Hub session client 1.4.0, plain JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio; Node 24 + Playwright 1.63 for tests |\n| Deliverable | one self-contained HTML file in the root, plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n\n**The core loop, in one sentence:** get the ball, beat your defender with turbo, spin moves and passes, and finish with a\ndunk that leaves the atmosphere or a jumper released at the top of the jump; on defense swipe, swat and shove (it's\nlegal); score three straight and you go **SUPERNOVA**.\n\n**Pillars** (use them to settle any question this plan doesn't):\n1. **Every possession can end in spectacle.** Dunks, blocks, shoves and steals are big, readable and loud.\n2. **Simple buttons, real timing.** Four main buttons; skill lives in release timing, turbo management, alley-oop\n   timing and steal angles.\n3. **Personality everywhere.** Two hype announcers, trash talk, courts with twists, a crowd that reacts.\n4. **Couch first, 10 feet away.** Up to four on one screen, a TV-readable HUD, controller-first menus.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title, names and folders\n\n- **Title: BASKETBLAM.** Picked as TASK.md asks: fifty riffs on \"basketball\" were brainstormed and one was chosen with a\n  real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned 18). Do not re-pick it.\n- **SOURCE token** in file names: `Basketball`.\n- **Names for this TASK.md** (copy them exactly):\n  - dev folder: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/` (in the root, beside TASK.md)\n  - deliverable: `Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822.html` (in the root)\n  - exit interview: `exit-interview.json` (in the root, beside the HTML)\n\n  If your TASK.md names another model or timestamp, use your TASK.md's names with the same `Basketblam` and\n  `Basketball` tokens.\n- **Dev folder layout:**\n\n```\nBasketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822/\n  handoff/            pristine copy of my-3d2dge dist/handoff (docs, tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, asset tools)\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js our modified engine (every change marked /* BASKETBLAM: ... */)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js       AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n  vendor/hub-harness/           harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only, never bundled)\n  src/*.js            the game, as numbered fragments (§4.2)\n  tools/build.mjs     concatenates src into build/game.js, writes build/index.html and the root deliverable\n  tests/run.mjs       the one test command (§19); tests/*.test.mjs; tests/helpers.mjs; tests/fakehub.mjs\n  build/              generated: game.js, index.html (dev page), hub/ (served for harness tests)\n  check-output/       screenshots and filmstrips for review (generated)\n  package.json        { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" } }\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. Genre, perspective and look\n\n**Genre: arcade sports, 2-on-2 basketball.** The over-the-top arcade style is defined by two-on-two, turbo, legal\ncontact, impossible dunks, a hot-streak power and loud personality. Those live best in short matches; a four-game story\nladder (the Tour) gives the demo an arc and story beats; Exhibition, local multiplayer and online show the rest.\n\n**Perspective: a side-on broadcast camera.** `const BROADCAST = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 30, 1, 1)`: yaw\n0, pitch 30°, one screen pixel per world unit. The court's length runs left to right (world x), the camera sits beyond\nthe near sideline looking across it, and pans horizontally with the ball. Why: the race up and down the court reads\nleft/right, dunks and blocks read in height at a low pitch (z is drawn at cos 30° = 0.87, the court's depth at\nsin 30° = 0.5), and below pitch 40 the engine draws rigs from the view itself, so jump poses read. The engine's\n`'threequarter'` view (pitch 55) is offered as Settings → Camera → High; every custom draw uses `r.w()` projection so\nboth views work (yaw is 0 in both, so the depth rules in §9 hold in both).\n\n**Look: the engine's native HD style, pushed to \"90s arcade broadcast\".** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; HD\nHumanoids at size 1.6–1.95 (players 48–56 world units tall, about 42–48 px on screen); textured court floors painted by\n`floorTex`; an `E.Backdrop` plus hand-drawn silhouettes behind every court; 3–8 props per screen; canvas lighting on\nevery court; `E.font.title` logos and comic banners (\"BLAM!\", \"REJECTED!\"); particles, hit-stop, shake and flashes on\nevery impact; broadcast graphics (a score bug, lower-third captions with live portraits). Saturated team colors, neon\nat the night courts, chunky outlines. Compare screenshots with PS1-era 2D, not NES.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and setup\n\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** (planned against repo commit `4390a985`, 2026-11-03), the full readable engine from\n  `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` (it already contains every module, including `arcade`). We change it where the game needs\n  (§5). If the repository has moved on, read the newer handoff `README.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` head for breaking changes.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`lib/hub/session.js`, ai-hub commit `f8835e48`), pasted\n  unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`.\n- **Code:** plain JavaScript in one IIFE (`const E = My3D2dge` first), no frameworks, no runtime network requests, no\n  CDN at runtime (the hub forbids network traffic outside the hub; the bundle must be self-contained).\n- **Tests:** Node 24 (`node:vm` headless runs), Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium (the handoff's pin) for browser checks,\n  `python3 -m http.server` for the hub harness.\n\n**Setup (from the root folder):**\n\n```sh\nDEV=Basketblam-Basketball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174822\nmkdir -p $DEV && cd $DEV\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git .engine-src\ngit -C .engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff && cp -r .engine-src/dist/handoff handoff && rm -rf .engine-src\nmkdir -p engine vendor/hub-harness && cp handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js\nB=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub\ncurl -fsSL $B/session.js -o vendor/aihub-session.js\nfor f in harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do curl -fsSL $B/$f -o vendor/hub-harness/$f; done\nnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm pkg set type=module && npm install -D playwright@1.63.0\nnpx playwright install chromium     # skip if ~/.cache/ms-playwright already has a matching chromium\n```\n\n**Read before coding** (in `handoff/`): `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `kits/sports.js` (whole: our starting point for\nthe ball, passes, `E.arc` lobs, formations, `allowJoin`, the proof and the bot), `game/test.mjs` and `kits/test.mjs`\n(headless testing), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before writing any shape), and grep `API-INDEX.md` for names.\n`vendor/hub-harness/README.md` is the hub protocol: read it whole before §15's milestone.\n\n**Environment rules** (from TASK.md, made concrete):\n- Read only what you need; other agents share the parent directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` pages and the engine's headless tools. The hub harness needs a server: bind\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (the OS picks the port; parse it from the server's first line), fetch\n  `/game.html` and check its `<title>` is Basketblam's before trusting a result, and stop only that server, by its PID.\n  Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n- /tmp was 99% full on this machine while this plan was written. If npm, git or Playwright fail with `ENOSPC`, export\n  `TMPDIR=$PWD/.tmp` (inside the dev folder) and delete that folder when done.\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Shape of the program\n\nOne `E.Game`, a set of scenes, and game systems that are plain objects working on one match state. The match\nsimulation never reads devices directly: every player slot reads a **command** each step from an **input source**\n(local device, CPU brain, network, attract director). That one rule gives local multiplayer, AI teammates, the attract\nmode, the dev mode's autopilot, the test bots and online play for the same code.\n\n```js\nconst HUB = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active);               // true from the first line inside a hub session\nconst game = new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: BROADCAST, views: ['broadcast', 'threequarter'],\n  bg: '#05040a', id: 'basketblam', input: KEYS, autoPause: !HUB,\n  menu: HUB ? false : { items: pauseItems, settings: GAME_SETTINGS, controls: CONTROL_NAMES, quit: 'title' } });\n```\n\n`pauseItems` is a function returning the list for the current context (the Shell evaluates `menu.items` when the pause\nscreen opens and skips falsy entries; confirm in `Shell` around \"`name === 'pause'`\" and patch the engine if it does\nnot). Items: `How to Play`, `Restart Game` (matches only), `Skip Cutscene` (story only), `Dev Mode`.\n\n### 4.2 Modules (src fragments, concatenated in this order into one IIFE)\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `E` alias, constants (§6.1), `HUB` flag, the `game`, input preset `KEYS`, settings specs, the shared state `S`, the event bus, RNG streams, registries (`def`, `get`, `all`) |\n| `01-tuning.js` | `TUNING` defaults, live values and slider metadata (Appendix A); `tune(key)` reads; `resetTuning()` |\n| `02-data-teams.js` | teams, players (ratings, rig specs, voices, taunts, celebrations), NPCs |\n| `03-data-courts.js` | courts (palette, floor style, backdrop, props, crowd, lights, music, twist), arena layout |\n| `04-data-story.js` | the Tour, story beats (scripts), matchup trash talk, halftime gags, sponsor cards, credits and About text |\n| `05-data-lines.js` | announcer line banks by event |\n| `06-data-moves.js` | animation clips, dunk catalog, taunts, power-ups, cheat codes |\n| `10-audio-music.js` | songs, stings, dynamic layers, crowd ambience |\n| `11-audio-sfx.js` | sound definitions (`audio.define`), material-aware dribble, sound policy |\n| `12-audio-voice.js` | the gibberish voice synth (§12.3) and its Dialog/caption hookup |\n| `20-court-build.js` | arena TileMap, floor painting (lines, logos), stands, collision bounds, shapes for props |\n| `21-court-draw.js` | hoops (stanchion, cheated backboard, rim halves, net simulation, shatter), crowd, backdrop silhouettes, lights |\n| `22-twists.js` | Jackpot Spot, wind, oil slicks, eruptions and steam vents, Dome pyro, boss mechanics |\n| `30-ball.js` | ball states, flight, floor/rim/backboard collisions, scoring detection, net contact, trails |\n| `31-player.js` | player entity: body, ratings, state machine, turbo, juice, power-up effects |\n| `32-anim.js` | rig factory (dressing), pose layer over `solvePose`, clips, dribble sync, dunk choreography |\n| `33-actions.js` | shot model, passes and alley-oops, steals, shoves, spin moves, blocks, goaltending, rebounds |\n| `34-ai.js` | CPU brains (offense, defense, rebounding, teammate commands), difficulty, comeback |\n| `35-match.js` | match flow (tip-off, quarters, clocks, possession, inbounds, overtime), Supernova, shatter pause, power-up spawner, stats, style points |\n| `36-fx.js` | camera director, dunk cam, banners, particle recipes, rumble, comfort-setting policy |\n| `40-ui-kit.js` | 10-foot widgets (focus, cards, buttons, sliders, toggles, pips), glyph icons, prompt lines, mouse hover/click |\n| `41-hud.js` | score bug, player plates, indicators, captions, warnings |\n| `42-screens.js` | title, main menu, join, team/lineup/court select, matchup card, quarter breaks, results, Hall of Blam, How to Play, About/Credits, touch-only notice |\n| `43-tour.js` | tour map, cutscene runner, tour progress and saves, ending, unlocks |\n| `44-camp.js` | Blam Camp lessons |\n| `45-attract.js` | idle detection and the attract director |\n| `46-dev.js` | dev panel, overlays, scene jumps, cheats |\n| `50-hub.js` | AI Hub integration (card, host, client, server, leave, time-up, end) |\n| `90-start.js` | scene table, boot steps, deep links, `game.debug`, `window.__bb`, `game.start` |\n\nRules: content never edits systems (systems read registries); systems talk through the event bus; anything a test or\nthe dev panel needs is reachable from `window.__bb`.\n\n### 4.3 One step (1/120 s) of a match\n\n1. **Commands:** for each of the 4 player slots, its input source writes a `Cmd` (below).\n2. **Players:** state machines consume commands; locomotion; actions start (shots, passes, steals, shoves, spins,\n   jumps); bodies move (`E.Body.update` against the arena map); rigs update (§7).\n3. **Ball:** attached (held/dribbled) or simulated (substeps near rims), collisions, catches, scoring.\n4. **Match:** clocks, possession, dead balls, inbounds, quarter ends, Supernova bookkeeping, spawner, twists.\n5. **Events** emitted during 1–4 are delivered (announcer, audio, crowd, stats, fx, attract director, net).\n6. **Camera director** sets focus, zoom and time scale.\n\n`Cmd` = `{ move: [x, y], shoot: { down, pressed, released }, pass: {...}, turbo: { down }, shove: {...}, oop: {...},\ntaunt: { pressed } }`. Sources: `LocalSource(input)` (from `game.players[i]`), `CpuSource(brain)`, `NetSource(seat)`\n(§15), `ScriptSource(steps)` (tests, attract). Swapping a slot's source is one assignment (dev autopilot, a player\nleaving a hub round, a player joining Exhibition).\n\n### 4.4 State\n\n`S` holds: `scene`, `mode` (`'exhibition' | 'tour' | 'camp' | 'attract' | 'hub'`), `court`, `teams[2]` (team id,\nlineup, bench), `players[4]` (slot 0–1 HOME, 2–3 AWAY), `ball`, `match` (phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score,\npossession, alternating arrow, streaks, stats, styleScore), `twist` state, `powerups`, `cheats`, `rng`, `fxRng`,\n`events` (ring buffer of the last 64 for debug and the net), `settings` mirror, `progress` (tour, unlocks, records).\n`serializeMatch()` and `applySnapshot()` (§15) live beside it from the first milestone, with a test.\n\n### 4.5 Events\n\n`emit(type, data)` / `on(type, fn)`. Types: `tipoff, possession, inbound, dribble, pass, catch, intercept, shotStart,\nrelease, rim, backboard, basket, miss, airball, block, goaltend, steal, fumble, shove, knockdown, spin, anklesBroken,\nrebound, putback, dunkStart, dunk, posterized, alleyoop, supernovaOn, supernovaOff, shatter, sponsorBreak, powerupSpawn,\npowerupPick, powerupEnd, jackpot, twist (with kind), shotClockViolation, quarterEnd, halftime, overtime, gameEnd,\ntaunt, cheat`. Each carries ids, positions and context (`points`, `dunkId`, `dist`, `contested`, `perfect`...).\n\n### 4.6 Randomness and determinism\n\n- `S.rng = E.rng(seed)`: every gameplay decision (shot outcomes, AI choices, steals, spawns, twist timing).\n- `S.fxRng`: cosmetic choices (crowd, which line the announcer says). Particles may use `Math.random`.\n- Tests seed `Math.random` too (as the kits' `proof()` does) so the rigs and particles repeat.\n- Same seed + same commands = same match. A `proof(n)` function hashes the state after n scripted steps (copy the\n  shell's `proof()` from `kits/sports.js`).\n\n### 4.7 Tuning\n\n`TUNING` is the single home of the important numbers (Appendix A): `{ key: { v, def, min, max, step, group, label,\nhelp } }`. Systems read `tune('runSpeed')` every time (never cache), so a dev slider changes play at once.\n`resetTuning()` restores `def`. Dev mode can save the current values to `E.store('bb:tuning')` and copy them as JSON.\n\n### 4.8 Persistence\n\n`E.store` keys: `bb:progress` (`{ tour: { team, stop, score, cheatsUsed, beatsSeen }, unlocked: { bots, allstars,\nbigHeads }, camp: [done lesson ids], records: { mostPoints, bestDunk, toursWon } }`), `bb:tuning`. The Tour also uses\n`game.saves` autosave (`write`/`read`, `autosave()` after each stop, `latest()` for CONTINUE TOUR). Hall of Blam\ntables use `E.Arcade` (`id: 'basketblam'`, mode `'tour'`). In hub sessions the client gives the game an empty\nin-memory storage: nothing to do.\n\n### 4.9 Deep links and the debug handle\n\nDeep links (the page's `#hash`, read in `90-start.js`): `#title`, `#attract`, `#exhibition/<court>/<home>-<away>`,\n`#tour/<stop 1-4>`, `#story/<beatId>`, `#camp/<lessonId>`, `#results`, `#credits`, `#about`, `#dev` (an exhibition with\nthe dev panel open), `#animlab`, `#shootaround`. `#animlab` (every clip on a rig, slow motion, scrub) and\n`#shootaround` (rim physics bench) are dev tools, also reachable from the dev panel's SCENES tab.\n\n`game.debug = { snapshot(), teleport(slot, x, y), give(slot), setScore(a, b), setClock(s), setQuarter(q), force(name,\nargs), go(scene, data), spawnPowerup(id, x, y), cheat(name, on), tune(key, v) }`. `snapshot()` returns `{ scene, mode,\ncourt, phase, quarter, clock, shotClock, score, possession, ball: { x, y, z, state, owner }, players: [{ slot, id, team,\nx, y, z, state, turbo, juice, nova, powerup, source }], lastEvents: [10 types], ui: { screen, focus }, attract:\n{ segment }, net: { role, seat, rtt } }`. `window.__bb = { S, game, TUNING, emit, on, debug: game.debug }`.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Engine changes (planned)\n\nMake each in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, mark it `/* BASKETBLAM: why */`, and list it on the About screen and in the exit\ninterview's engine feedback.\n\n1. **Basketball shorts on `Humanoid`:** option `shorts: { len: .55, sock: '#ffffff', sockLen: .35 }`. In the HD leg\n   drawing (`limb(hip, knee, ..., C.pants)`, `limb(knee, ank, ...)`, about line 4388) draw the thigh in `C.pants` only\n   down to `len` of its length (shorts with a hem in `C.trim`), the rest of the thigh and the shin in `C.skin`, and the\n   last `sockLen` of the shin in `sock`; the same in the classic path (about line 4576).\n2. **Hair styles** `'afro'` (a big shaded ball around the back and top of the head), `'flattop'` (a tall flat box),\n   `'mohawk'` (a ridge of spikes front to back) in the HD head drawing beside `'spiky'` (about line 4493), and in\n   `drawPortrait`.\n3. **`Dialog.voice` may be a function** `(letter, index, pageText)` called for each letter typed (about line 8956),\n   so the gibberish voice speaks the line as it types.\n4. **Public audio buses:** `audio.sfxBus` and `audio.musicBus` getters for `_sfxBus` / `_musBus`, so the voice and crowd\n   nodes follow the volume settings.\n5. Any engine bug found while building: fix it in the engine (not around it), with a test, and log it for the\n   exit interview.\n\nBig heads need no engine change: `rig.o.headR` is read when drawing, so the cheat multiplies it (keep the original to\nrestore it).\n\n---\n\n## 6. Court, rules and core mechanics\n\n### 6.1 Court geometry (world units; one tile = 16; z up)\n\nArena map: 48 × 28 tiles (768 × 448). Court-local coordinates `(cx, cy)`: x 0..640 baseline to baseline, y 0..288 far\nsideline to near sideline. World = court-local + `(64, 112)`. Constants (court-local):\n\n| Thing | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Court | 640 × 288; centre (320, 144) |\n| Rim centres | (52, 144) and (588, 144); z 80; inner radius 9; tube radius 1.2 |\n| Backboards (collision) | planes x = 40 and x = 600, y 120..168, z 72..100 |\n| Ball | radius 4.5 |\n| Three-point line | arc radius 136 from the rim centre; straight corner lines at y = 20 and y = 268 from the baseline to x = 108 (mirrored at the other end) |\n| Key (paint) | y 96..192, baseline to x = 152 (free-throw line); free-throw circle r 48 |\n| Centre circle | r 40; half-court line x = 320 |\n| Out of bounds | none: invisible walls 24 units outside the lines stop players; the ball bounces off them (the crowd) |\n| Players | 48–56 tall; body radius 6; standing reach about z 59 (size 1.7) |\n\nNorth of the court (screen top): an apron (16) and stands of 4 tiers (heights 12/24/36/48) with the crowd. West and\neast: 64 of apron with the stanchion bases, benches, props. South (camera side): 48 of apron with low props (scorer's\ntable, cameras) drawn with `cut` so they never hide play. One player is controlled by each human; there is no switching\nof players (the genre's rule).\n\n### 6.2 Controls and actions\n\n| Action | Pad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse | With the ball | Without the ball |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| move | left stick, d-pad | WASD (arrows for a lone player too) | – | run / dribble | run, defend |\n| `shoot` | □ Square (`Pad2`) | Space, J | left button | hold: jump; release at the top to shoot; in dunk range with turbo: dunk | jump to block or rebound; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"shoot it!\" |\n| `pass` | ✕ Cross (`Pad0`) | E, K | right button | pass to your teammate (led) | steal swipe; if your CPU teammate has the ball: \"pass it to me!\" |\n| `turbo` | R2 (`Pad7`), R1 (`Pad5`) | Shift (left or right) | – | hold: faster, higher, dunks | hold: faster, higher |\n| `shove` | ○ Circle (`Pad1`) | Q, L | – | SPIN MOVE | SHOVE |\n| `oop` | △ Triangle (`Pad3`) | F, I | – | alley-oop lob to your teammate | call for the ball; CPU teammate with the ball throws you an oop if you're in range |\n| `taunt` | L1 (`Pad4`) | R, U | – | – | taunt (dead balls or without the ball; off in hub sessions) |\n| `pause` / menus | Options, ✕/○ | Esc/P, Enter/Esc | click, right-click back | | |\n\nPreset: `const KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, { shoot: ['Space', 'KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad2'], pass: ['KeyE',\n'KeyK', 'Mouse2', 'Pad0'], turbo: ['ShiftLeft', 'ShiftRight', 'Pad7', 'Pad5'], shove: ['KeyQ', 'KeyL', 'Pad1'], oop:\n['KeyF', 'KeyI', 'Pad3'], taunt: ['KeyR', 'KeyU', 'Pad4'] })`, minus `attack, dash, skill, jump`. Names for the Controls\nscreen: `{ shoot: 'Shoot / Block', pass: 'Pass / Steal', turbo: 'Turbo', shove: 'Shove / Spin', oop: 'Alley-oop / Call',\ntaunt: 'Taunt' }`. Two players on one keyboard get the engine's halves (`E.Input.halfKeys`): check what it assigns and\nshow it on the join screen. Combo shortcut kept for genre veterans: turbo + pass without the ball also shoves.\n\n### 6.3 Match flow and rules (\"What you can and can't do\")\n\n- **2 on 2, full court, 4 quarters.** Quarter length: Exhibition from Settings (1:00 / 1:30 / 2:00 / 3:00, default\n  2:00); Tour 1:30; hub 2 halves of 1:30. The game clock runs only while the ball is live; it stops on made baskets\n  until the inbound is caught, and during breaks.\n- **Tip-off** starts the game and each overtime: the two jumpers (highest SWAT on each side) at centre; REF ROSCOE\n  tosses the ball (apex z 120 at 0.9 s); press shoot to jump; the jump whose hands peak nearest the ball's apex wins\n  (CPU timing error by difficulty) and taps it to the teammate. Quarters 2–4 begin with an inbound at half court by the\n  alternating arrow (the team that lost the tip gets Q2 and Q3).\n- **Scoring:** 2 points; 3 from beyond the arc (by the shooter's takeoff point); dunks 2; Jackpot Spot +2; Gold Ball\n  doubles. A shot released before the buzzer counts.\n- **No fouls.** Shoving, steals and body contact are legal. No traveling, no backcourt, no three seconds, no out of\n  bounds (the walls keep everything in).\n- **Shot clock 20 s;** resets on a change of possession and when a shot touches the rim. Expiry: buzzer, turnover,\n  inbound by the other team from the nearest sideline.\n- **Goaltending:** touching the ball while it is coming down (vz < 0) within 18 units of the rim centre and above the\n  rim counts the basket for the shooter (\"GOALTEND!\"). Supernova players and BLAMBOTS ignore this rule.\n- **After a made basket** the other team inbounds from under the basket it defends: the inbounder passes in after 0.8 s\n  (or when its human presses pass). Defenders may not stand within 24 units of the inbounder; the inbound pass can be\n  intercepted only by a defender standing in its lane.\n- **Tie after Q4:** overtime of 1:00, again if tied; after two overtimes, SUDDEN BLAM: the next basket wins (so every\n  game ends, which the tests rely on).\n- **Breaks:** after Q1 and Q3 a short break card (6 s, subs allowed); after Q2 the Halftime Report (stats, a gag, subs).\n\n### 6.4 Ratings\n\nEach player has eight ratings, 1–10, shown as pips with icons (never raw numbers on the 10-foot screens):\n\n| Rating | Means | Effect (r = rating) |\n|---|---|---|\n| ZOOM | speed | run speed × (0.86 + 0.028 r) |\n| SPLASH | two-point jumpers, layups | make chance × (0.78 + 0.045 r) |\n| DEEP | three-pointers | make chance × (0.70 + 0.06 r) |\n| SLAM | dunking | dunk range × (0.75 + 0.05 r); unlocks dunks (§10.3); resists blocks |\n| DIME | passing, handles | pass speed 180 + 12 r; interception window; ball security vs steals; alley-oop range |\n| SWIPE | steals | steal chance × (0.55 + 0.09 r); steal reach 14 + 0.6 r |\n| SWAT | blocks, rebounds | jump speed 250 + 10 r; block reach 10 + 0.8 r; block chance |\n| BUMP | power | shove force 140 + 14 r; knockdown time; fumble chance; shove resistance; box-outs |\n\nEvery effect is multiplied by the juice factor (§6.14) and by power-ups and Supernova where they apply.\n\n### 6.5 Movement and turbo\n\n- Velocity approaches `stick × runSpeed × ZOOM factor` at `accel` (900 u/s²). Facing turns toward the movement; on\n  defense with the opponent holding the ball, the defender faces the ball handler and shuffles (defensive stance).\n- Bodies are `E.Body` (radius 6, gravity `playerGravity` 900) on the arena TileMap; players push each other apart\n  (separation, no damage); `E.SpatialHash` is unnecessary at four players.\n- **Turbo** (0..1 per player): held and moving drains `turboDrain` 0.30/s; not held refills `turboRefill` 0.20/s after\n  `turboRefillDelay` 0.35 s; speed × `turboMult` 1.5, jumps × 1.15; required to start a dunk (≥ 0.05); a shove costs\n  0.25, a spin move 0.2. Supernova and Rocket Kicks change it (§6.12, §6.15).\n- A sharp change of direction at speed squeaks the sneakers and kicks dust.\n\n### 6.6 The ball\n\nStates: `held` (by its owner's hands), `dribble` (owner; position from the dribble cycle, §7), `pass` (flying to a\ntarget), `shot` (flying with its shot record: shooter, points, decided outcome, release time, perfect flag), `loose`\n(free physics), `dead` (scripted: after a score, during breaks), `inbound`.\n\n- Gravity `ballGravity` 760; floor bounce restitution `ballBounce` 0.62 with friction; rolling decays.\n- **Rim:** a torus (ring radius 9, tube 1.2). Closest point Q on the ring circle to the ball's centre; if\n  `|ball − Q| < 4.5 + 1.2` reflect the velocity along the normal with `rimBounce` 0.45 and a little friction; the rim\n  \"rattles\" (visual bend + clank). Substep the ball 4× (1/480 s) within 40 units of a rim; never let it tunnel.\n- **Backboard:** the plane x = 40 (or 600) within its y/z bounds; restitution 0.55; a thud.\n- **Arena walls:** 24 outside the lines; restitution 0.5.\n- **Basket:** the ball's centre crosses z = 80 downward within 6.75 of the rim centre: score once (a `scored` flag\n  holds until it falls below z 60); the net slows it (vz × 0.6) as it passes.\n- Players do not collide with the ball except to catch, steal, block, tip or goaltend it.\n\n### 6.7 Shooting\n\nPressing shoot with the ball (out of dunk range, or without turbo) starts a **jump shot**: gather 0.08 s, jump\n(`jumpShotVz` 250 + SWAT bonus), the ball rises above the forehead. Releasing the button releases the ball; holding past\nthe top releases it automatically 0.25 s after the top (a late release). The **release meter** (a thin arc around the\nshooter, Settings → Shot meter, on by default) fills toward the top of the jump; the PERFECT window is `perfectWindow`\n0.10 s wide, centred on the top.\n\nThe outcome is decided at release, then flown physically:\n\n```\np = base(dist) × ratingFactor × release × contest × shotSkill × (3PT ? threeSkill : 1) × juice + bonuses\nbase(dist): dist ≤ 40: 0.85 · 40–80: 0.85→0.70 · 80–136: 0.70→0.45 · 136–200: 0.38→0.30 · 200–320: 0.30→0.08 · beyond: 0.03\nratingFactor: SPLASH (2PT) or DEEP (3PT) factor (§6.4)\nrelease: PERFECT × (1 + perfectBonus 0.15) · inside 2× the window × 1 · early or late × (1 − badReleasePenalty 0.30)\ncontest: nearest defender between shooter and rim within 30 and airborne × (1 − contestPenalty 0.40); within 30 on the floor × 0.80; within 50 × 0.92\nbonuses (added): Supernova + novaShotBonus 0.25 · Laser Sight + 0.30 · comeback assist (§8, Comeback assist)\nmodifiers: turbo held × 1.05 · drifting sideways fast × 0.90 · wind (the Pier) × (1 − 0.35 × |wind| × dist / 320)\nclamp to [0.02, 0.97]\n```\n\n`made = S.rng() < p`. The flight is solved with `E.arc(release, target, ballGravity, { apex })`, apex = max(release z,\nrim z) + clamp(18 + dist × 0.10, 18, 60) × `shotArc`. Made: the target is the rim centre 2 below the rim plane, entering\non the way down (a swish); 1 in 4 makes target a point 3 off the centre and are helped in if they touch the rim (a\n\"rattle in\": nudge the velocity toward the centre). Missed: the target is a point on the front rim, back rim, side rim or\nthe backboard, chosen by the error, so the ball bounces off naturally; if physics drops a decided miss in anyway (rare)\nit counts (it's a real basket). A miss by more than 30 units from the rim is an **airball**. Wind adds a constant\nhorizontal acceleration during flight: solve the launch with it included so the ball curves and still reaches the\ndecided target. Tests hold made rate to the decided rate within 3% (§19).\n\n### 6.8 Layups, dunks, alley-oops, putbacks\n\n- **Layup:** shoot pressed within 40 units of the rim without turbo, or with turbo when the player can't dunk: a short\n  one-handed rise and scoop; make chance from the 0..40 band (high); blockable.\n- **Dunk:** shoot pressed with turbo held inside the dunk range: `dunkRange` 150 × SLAM factor (Supernova + 40, Moon\n  Shoes to the three-point line + 20, steam vent: anywhere in that half). The dunk is chosen from the catalog (§10.3) by\n  distance, approach angle, SLAM rating and context (weighted by `S.rng`). Flight: from takeoff to the slam point (the\n  feet where the dunking hand reaches 6 above the rim; about feet z 28 at size 1.7) with `E.arc(…, dunkGravity 520,\n  { apex: slamFeetZ + apexAbove × dunkApex })`, time × `dunkHang`. The ball rides the hands (§7); at the slam the ball is\n  forced down through the hoop (a made basket), the rim bends and snaps back, and the dunker either drops or hangs.\n  A dunk scores unless it is **blocked** before the slam: a defender in the air whose block hands meet the ball, with\n  chance `clamp(0.25 + 0.06 × (SWAT_def − SLAM_off) + timing, 0.05, 0.75) × blockChance`. Players with SLAM ≤ 3 miss\n  4% of dunks off the back rim (comedy).\n- **Alley-oop:** the ball handler presses `oop` while the teammate is within the oop range (dunk range × 1.15 + DIME\n  × 4) of the rim and not covered tightly: the teammate leaps (dunk flight to the rim) and the passer lobs to meet the\n  hands at 70% of the flight (`E.arc` with the flight's time). A defender in the lob's path can intercept (steal), one\n  in the flight's path can block. Out of range, `oop` throws a plain lob to the teammate. A human receiver whose CPU\n  teammate has the ball presses `oop` to ask for it.\n- **Putback:** catching an offensive rebound in the air within 40 units of the rim, then pressing shoot (or holding\n  turbo) slams it at once (\"PUTBACK!\").\n- **Posterized:** a dunk slammed within 24 units of a defender in front of the rim knocks that defender down with dizzy\n  stars (\"POSTERIZED!\").\n\n### 6.9 Passing\n\n`pass` with the ball: a chest pass at chest height along the floor plane to the teammate, led by the receiver's velocity\n(`kits/sports.js`'s `pass()`), speed `180 + 12 × DIME` × 1.6; a bounce pass when a defender's hands are near the lane\n(cosmetic: one floor bounce). The receiver catches when the ball passes within 12 of its hands. Interception: a defender\nwithin 10 of the ball's line, chance by SWIPE vs DIME (0.15 to 0.55). Commands to a CPU teammate holding the ball:\n`pass` = pass to me, `shoot` = shoot now, `oop` = throw me an oop.\n\n### 6.10 Defense: steals, shoves, spin moves, blocks\n\n- **Steal** (`pass` on defense): a 0.2 s swipe, lunge 6, reach `stealReach` 18 (+ SWIPE). Chance per attempt:\n  `stealChance 0.30 × SWIPE factor × (1.25 − 0.05 × DIME_handler) × angle × context`; angle × 1.4 from behind or the\n  dribble-hand side, × 0.6 from the off-hand front; handler on turbo × 1.15; handler spinning or in a shot: 0. Success\n  pops the ball loose toward the stealer (or straight to its hands within 8). Cooldown `stealCooldown` 0.6 s; a miss\n  leaves a 0.2 s recovery.\n- **Shove** (`shove` on defense, or turbo + pass): wind 0.06 s, a two-handed push with lunge 10, reach 22, ±60° arc;\n  cost 0.25 turbo (needs ≥ 0.1), cooldown `shoveCooldown` 1.0 s. A hit knocks the target down for\n  `knockdownTime 0.8 + 0.04 × (BUMP_att − BUMP_def)` (0.3..1.4 s), sliding back with `shoveForce` 220 × BUMP factor.\n  A ball handler fumbles with chance `shoveFumble 0.6 + 0.04 × (BUMP_att − BUMP_def)` (0.2..0.95); a shooter in the air\n  loses the shot with 0.7. Shoving costs the target juice.\n- **Spin move** (`shove` with the ball): a 0.35 s 360° spin dribble (`rig.spin`) carrying the handler 30 forward;\n  steals fail during it; a defender within 14 in front stumbles 0.4 s (\"ANKLES BROKEN!\"). Cost 0.2 turbo; cooldown 1.5 s.\n- **Block** (`shoot` on defense): a jump (`250 + 10 × SWAT`, × 1.15 with turbo) with both arms up; during the jump a\n  swat swing triggers when the ball comes within `blockReach` (14 + SWAT); against a shot in its first 0.25 s of flight,\n  a layup or a dunk before the slam it succeeds with the block chance; a block sends the ball flying (toward half court\n  if possible) and emits `block` (\"REJECTED!\"). Touching a descending shot is goaltending (§6.3).\n\n### 6.11 Rebounds and loose balls\n\nA missed shot that touches the rim or board is `loose`. Everyone can catch a loose ball within reach (horizontal 10,\nvertical from the feet to the standing reach, plus the jump). `shoot` near a loose ball in the air jumps for it\n(rebound). Box-out: a player with higher BUMP between an opponent and the ball holds position (the other is nudged\naside). Loose balls on the floor are picked up by touching them.\n\n### 6.12 SUPERNOVA\n\n- **Ignites** when one player scores `novaBaskets` 3 baskets in a row for the team with no basket by the other team in\n  between. The HUD shows each player's streak as three heat pips.\n- **While on:** unlimited turbo, + `novaShotBonus` 0.25 to every shot, + 40 dunk range, + 20% steal chance, goaltending\n  allowed, dunks shatter the glass with 35%, the ball leaves a comet trail of star-fire (`r.actor` ghosts on the player,\n  glints and embers on the ball), the rim glows, the crowd chants, the music adds its \"nova\" layer.\n- **Ends** when the other team scores, after `novaMaxBaskets` 4 more baskets, or after `novaTime` 40 s of game clock.\n\n### 6.13 Backboard shatter\n\nA dunk shatters the backboard with chance 0 normally, 3% for SLAM ≥ 9 turbo dunks, 35% in Supernova, 50% with Moon\nShoes, 20% for the Volcano Launch, 100% for BLAM-QUAKE (all × `shatterChance`), at most twice a game. The basket counts;\nglass shards (`particles.bits` in pale blue and white, glints) rain; hit-stop 0.15, flash, slow motion 0.3 for 0.5 s;\nthen the **SPONSOR BREAK**: a 4-second fake commercial card (\"WE'LL BE RIGHT BACK AFTER THIS MESSAGE FROM GLOBO-BLAM\nGLASS: NOW 40% LESS SHATTERY!\") with its jingle, while a cleaning bot rolls out and fits new glass; then the inbound.\n\n### 6.14 Juice and substitutions\n\nEach player has juice 0..1 (starts 1). It drops 0.06 per knockdown suffered, 0.01 per second of turbo, 0.004 per second\non court (× `fatigue`); a break restores 0.25 to players on court and 0.5 to the bench. The juice factor\n`0.85 + 0.15 × juice` multiplies the ratings' effects. Each team carries 3 players: at each break, a human picks who\nplays (the break screen shows juice bars); CPU teams bench the lowest juice. Hub rounds have no subs.\n\n### 6.15 Power-ups and the Jackpot Spot\n\nPower-ups appear in Exhibition (Settings → Power-ups, default on), on Tour stops 2–4 and the final, in attract mode and\nin hub rounds. One on the floor at a time (two in attract), every `powerupInterval` 22 s (± 6), lasting 10 s, never on\na twist hazard; run over it to take it; its effect shows as an icon and a countdown on the player's plate.\n\n| Id | Name | Effect | Lasts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `rocket` | ROCKET KICKS | speed + 40%, turbo refills twice as fast | 15 s |\n| `glue` | GLUE GLOVES | steals always succeed against players without Glue Gloves; you can't be stripped | 15 s |\n| `laser` | LASER SIGHT | + 0.30 shot chance; the PERFECT window doubles | next 2 shots or 15 s |\n| `moon` | MOON SHOES | dunk range to the three-point line + 20; jumps × 1.5; dunks shatter 50% | 15 s |\n| `bomb` | BLAM BOMB | your next shove is a shockwave: knocks down every opponent within 60, always fumbles | one use, 20 s |\n| `gold` | GOLD BALL | your team's next basket counts double | until used, 30 s |\n\n**Jackpot Spot** (the Skybox, and Exhibition with Settings → Jackpot Spot on): a glowing ring (radius 16) appears at a\nrandom place inside the three-point range of one basket for 20 s, then moves; a made jump shot taken with the feet in\nit scores + 2 (\"JACKPOT!\").\n\n### 6.16 Stats and style points\n\nPer player: PTS, FGM/FGA, 3PM/3PA, DUNKS, REB, AST, STL, BLK, KNOCKDOWNS, SHATTERS, NOVAS. **Style points** feed the\nTour score and the Hall of Blam: dunk 200 + the dunk's own bonus, three 150, alley-oop 250, block 100, steal 80,\nputback 150, posterized 200, shatter 1000, Supernova 300, jackpot 250, ankles broken 100; a win adds 2000 + 50 per\npoint of margin. A game played with cheats is marked ★ and can't enter the Hall of Blam.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Animation\n\nThe base is the engine's own: Humanoid rigs posed by math each step (`rig.update`), its locomotion, `air`, `flip`,\n`pose` (`cheer`, `hips`, `wave`, `guard`, `crouch`, `down`), `expr`, `hurt`, `land()`, `kick()` and attack specs. On\ntop we add a **pose layer** for everything basketball: hands that hold and dribble the ball, shooting form, dunk\nchoreography, hanging on the rim, celebrations and taunts.\n\n### 7.1 The pose layer\n\n```js\nconst base = rig.solvePose;\nrig.solvePose = function () { base.call(this); poseLayer(this, this.bb); };   // bb: the player's anim state\n```\n\n`poseLayer` reads the player's current clip and time, interpolates the clip's keys (eased), and for each hand with a\ntarget solves the arm with `E.ik3(shoulder, target, armUpper × size, armLower × size, elbowHint)`, writing\n`J.elbowR/handR` (and L). Targets are written `[forward, right, up]` in the rig's own frame at size 1 and multiplied by\n`rig.o.size`; check the units against how `solvePose` builds `J` (read it once) before writing clips. A clip may also\nset body tweaks (lean, a crouch amount, a hop) and a `pose` / `expr` it passes to `rig.update`. Spins rotate the\n`facing` given to `rig.update` (2π over the move); flips use the rig's `flip: 0..1`. The ball attaches to the hand(s):\nits world point is `rig.worldOffset(J.handR)` (or the mid-point of both hands) plus the rig's position, nudged 4.5 out\nof the palm.\n\n### 7.2 The dribble (procedural, synced to the ball)\n\nA phase φ in 0..1 advances at 2.2 Hz standing, 3.0 running, 3.8 on turbo. The ball falls from the hand (about hip\nheight) to the floor (contact at φ = 0.5, the dribble sound for the court's floor) and back up, as two parabolas; the\nhand is in contact for φ in [0.85, 1] ∪ [0, 0.15], pushing down at φ ≈ 0.05, and hovers above the ball otherwise. The\nball sits 5 forward and 6 to the dribble side (× size). **Crossover:** when the move direction turns more than 100° or a\ndefender reaches the dribble side within 20, the next bounce lands on the other side (the ball crosses in front) and\nthe hand switches. The off hand guards (forearm out toward the nearest defender).\n\n### 7.3 Clips (data in `06-data-moves.js`; targets at size 1)\n\n| Clip | Length | Hands and body |\n|---|---|---|\n| `hold` (triple threat) | loop | both hands at [4, ±1.5, 15], ball between; slight crouch |\n| `gather` | .08 s | ball to the chest [4, 0, 20] |\n| `jumpshot` | rise → release → follow | rise: right hand under the ball at [3, 1, 33], left at its side [2, −3, 30]; release (.1 s): right arm to [5, 1.5, 38]; the follow-through holds until landing |\n| `layup` | rise | right hand scoops [5, 2, 16] → [6, 2, 40]; left guards [3, −3, 26] |\n| `passChest` | .08 + .12 s | both hands [4, ±1.5, 19] → [11, ±1, 20] |\n| `passBounce` | .08 + .12 s | both hands [4, ±1.5, 18] → [10, ±1, 10] |\n| `passLob` (and oop lob, inbound) | .1 + .15 s | both hands [2, ±1.5, 22] → [6, ±1, 38] overhead |","numLines":577,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1605,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CwneqDbwLxPLfUFV3fta81","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
