{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxLivKt1bcJtNAHReHH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012tTcix8jkGLAHsLAqbB6Nj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -E \"^- \\`(new )?E\\.(View|TileMap|Humanoid|Body|Attack|Menu|Shell|Dialog|Game|Arcade|Blob|Platformer|Backdrop)\\b|^- \\`(game|input|audio|particles|lights|r|px)\\.[a-zA-Z]+\\(\" API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-260 | head -150","description":"Grep signatures of core engine constructors and methods"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4570,"cache_read_input_tokens":153060,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4570},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0432b045-5008-4207-9914-efcb595d2dad","uuid":"73b69850-cc11-4210-8528-c3a62d60cdaf","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:19:23.983Z","request_id":"req_011CghxLidTgef5MLSj7zHyo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_012tTcix8jkGLAHsLAqbB6Nj":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-061830__x5a4f79/work/Basketblam-Basketball-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-061830/handoff && grep -n -E \"^- \\`(new )?E\\.(View|TileMap|Humanoid|Body|Attack|Menu|Shell|Dialog|Game|Arcade|Blob|Platformer|Backdrop)\\b|^- \\`(game|input|audio|particles|lights|r|px)\\.[a-zA-Z]+\\(\" API-INDEX.md | cut -c1-260 | head -150","description":"Grep signatures of core engine constructors and methods"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_012tTcix8jkGLAHsLAqbB6Nj":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-061830__x5a4f79/work/Basketblam-Basketball-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-061830/handoff"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012tTcix8jkGLAHsLAqbB6Nj","type":"tool_result","content":"197:- `px.blend(g, a, mode, fn)`: draw fn() at alpha a (stepped, like PS1 hardware) with a blend mode: 'normal', 'add', 'multiply', 'screen' (E.style.trans = 'dither' dithers instead)\n198:- `px.col(g, c)`: set the fill color (px.* do it themselves)\n199:- `px.ddisc(g, cx, cy, r, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: soft disc that fades toward its edge (glows, smoke, dust): stepped alpha rings, or dithering in 'dither' style\n200:- `px.disc(g, cx, cy, r, c)`: a filled circle\n201:- `px.dot(g, x, y, c)`: one pixel\n202:- `px.ell(g, cx, cy, rx, ry, c)`: a filled ellipse\n203:- `px.glow(g, e = 1)`: GPU lighting: what follows glows (0..1). No effect in Canvas mode, so call it freely.\n204:- `px.line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, w = 1)`: a line w pixels thick, no smoothing\n205:- `px.poly(g, pts, c)`: a filled polygon through [[x, y], ...]\n206:- `px.polyDither(g, pts, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: translucent polygon: a = 0..1 opacity (stepped alpha, or a dither pattern in 'dither' style). wx/wy = world pixel offset for the pattern\n207:- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`: a filled rectangle, snapped to whole pixels\n208:- `px.reset(g)`: forget the color cache (after drawing on g with the canvas API directly)\n209:- `px.sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip = false)`: draw a sprite with its top-left corner at buffer pixel (x, y). flip mirrors it left-right; or o: { flip, rot (radians, turned about its center: E.rotSprite), steps, scale (nearest-neighbour) }\n223:- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n276:- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n277:- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\"<action>\" inside root to the action\n279:- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then \n280:- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\n281:- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n282:- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a \n283:- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\n284:- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\n285:- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\n286:- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\n287:- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\n288:- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n289:- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, lon\n291:- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\n292:- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or\n293:- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\n294:- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '62\n295:- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\n296:- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\n297:- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\n298:- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\n299:- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\n300:- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\n301:- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\n302:- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the co\n303:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false \n304:- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\n305:- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\n306:- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\n307:- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\n308:- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\n309:- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\n310:- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions ar\n311:- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n312:- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\n313:- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\n314:- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\n315:- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\n316:- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n317:- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { j\n318:- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('\n319:- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\n320:- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, S\n321:- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\n322:- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\n407:- `r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: Queue a character. drawFn(g, ox, oy) must draw with (ox, oy) as the projected position of world point (x, y, z). The engine adds outline, rim light, hit flash, afterimages and x-ray silhouettes. opts: {outline=true, ri\n408:- `r.actor({ xray })`: true shows the actor's hidden part (a translucent tint with a 1-px outline, r.xrayColor and r.xrayOutline; a checker in E.style.trans 'dither') wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high, or anything queued with hides is dr\n409:- `r.actor({ hides })`: true: this actor counts as an x-ray occluder even when no x-ray actor asked for actors\n410:- `r.actor({ ghost })`: ghost.owner (any {x, y, z}, usually the actor itself) keeps its afterimages sorted behind the live body\n411:- `r.actor({ every, key })`: every: n, key (any object, the character's own): its finished image is drawn again for n - 1 frames before drawFn runs again, at the place it is now (a background character animating at a lower rate, as classic games' townsfolk\n412:- `r.actor({ wrap })`: [w, h], as r.sprite's: a world that wraps round shows the actor across each edge it crosses\n413:- `r.actorNow(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: draw an actor at once, over what is drawn so far, not sorted by depth (a pass of the game's own: a body falling into a void painted over the level), with r.actor's options (outline, flash, alpha, ...)\n415:- `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)`: extruded box prop (crate, pedestal, table). Call inside a queue fn with its g.\n418:- `r.decal(fn, o)`: draw on the floor before the queue. opts.emissive (0..1) makes it glow under GPU lighting\n419:- `r.glowDisc(g, cx, cy, rad, color, a)`: glow halo from a cache: additive light rings (alpha style) or a dithered disc (dither style). a = 0..1 strength\n420:- `r.groundArc(x, y, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha = 1, z = 0, g = this.tgt)`: a band of a ring on the ground from radius r0 to r1 and angle a0 to a1 (radians): melee telegraphs, sight cones\n421:- `r.groundDisc(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a dithered disc lying on the ground (radius in world units, z its height): shadows, auras, area attacks\n422:- `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n = 18, z = 0)`: n screen points round a circle of radius rad on the ground at (x, y), height z: the outline of a ring or a fan\n423:- `r.groundRing(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a ring lying on the ground (radius in world units)\n424:- `r.marker(x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: an objective marker over a world point: a bobbing diamond (and its label) while it is on screen, an arrow at the screen's edge pointing to it while it is off (offscreen: false hides it then). o: { label, color: '#ffd040',\n425:- `r.overlay(fn)`: draw fn(g) on top of everything, in screen pixels (HUD, dialog, menus)\n426:- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit }))\n427:- `r.prop(name, x, y, z, o = {})` †: set dressing from E.prop: r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { size, color, flip, light: true, halo: true, bias, hides, footprint }) feet on the world point; flames animate and glow. light: true also adds a warm light to game.lig\n428:- `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, o)`: queue a depth-sorted draw. fn(g) draws into g. opts: {bias, occluder, hides, xray, emissive, solid (hides a glow behind it), footprint} occluder: a wall (x-ray silhouettes show through it, it sorts as a block). hides: only lets\n429:- `r.shadow(x, y, rad, alpha = .5, color = '#0c0818', z = 0)`: soft contact shadow under a character: a cached dithered ellipse, correct for the current view. z = height of the surface it falls on (0 = floor; the top of a block or platform otherwise)\n430:- `r.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: Queue a shape standing at (x, y, z), feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor: r.shape(id | entry, x, y, z, o). o: { facing (radians, where its front points; default PI/2, south, as written), scale, mats: { slot\n431:- `r.sightCone(obs, o = {})` †: an observer's view on the ground: a dithered fan fov wide (radians) about its facing, out to range, cut short where the map hides a body's chest (map.ray, the height rules), draped over the ground below. o: { range: 120, fov\n432:- `r.sky(colors, o = {})`: background of dithered color bands, top to bottom: r.sky(['#0b1030', '#3b2d6b', '#e0806a']). Call first in draw.\n433:- `r.sprite(x, y, z, spr, o = {})`: Queue a pixel sprite (from E.sprite) standing at world point (x, y, z). Depth sorted like an actor. opts: { flip, anchor: 'bottom' (feet on the point, default; E.shapeSprite's: its own feet) | 'center' | 'top', glow: 0..\n434:- `r.starfield(o = {})`: scrolling star dots for space and night skies. opts: { count: 70, speed: 30 px/s, dir: 'down' | 'up' | 'left' | 'right', colors, layers: 3, seed }\n435:- `r.text(str, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o)`: HUD text in screen pixels, drawn on top and never lit: r.text('SCORE 120', 4, 4, '#fff', { align, scale, outline })\n436:- `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: text floating at a world point (names, prompts): r.textAt(x, y, z, 'OPEN', '#ffe08a')\n439:- `r.visible(x, y, z = 0, mx = 60, up = 30, down = 90)`: is a world point near the visible area? margins in pixels (characters extend upward from their feet)\n440:- `r.w(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> buffer pixel coords\n453:- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause: true, id, pace, camera }). The standard features (sect\n454:- `new E.Game({ res })`: a fixed resolution ('ps1' or [w, h]), whole pixels\n455:- `new E.Game({ expand })`: true makes res the least the screen shows and grows the picture to fill the window, any shape, no bars (expand: { maxW, maxH } stops it growing there)\n456:- `new E.Game({ bg })`: the color behind everything, or null: the canvas is clear, and the game draws its HUD and menus over a picture under it (a 3D world, engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n457:- `new E.Game({ headless })`: no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input (tests, tools, a simulation in Node, where it is the default): scenes update and draw is skipped; drive it with advance() and input.press / release / tap / hold\n458:- `new E.Game({ loop })`: false makes a game in a page that runs only by advance()\n459:- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n460:- `new E.Game({ fullscreen })`: 'tap' (a phone goes full screen at its first tap and when a game starts, unless the player turned it off), 'button' (only when asked) or false\n461:- `new E.Game({ corner, backGesture })`: corner: the menu and full-screen buttons (top right); backGesture: a phone's back steps back in the game first (false: it leaves the page)\n462:- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n463:- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n464:- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n465:- `new E.Game({ camera })`: the camera of every scene without its own (a scene's camera option: the player's zoom, start)\n468:- `game.addPlayer(o = {})` †: a player in now: { pad: index } (a gamepad) or { keys: 'right' } (the keyboard's right half; player 1 takes the left). Returns its input (null when four play already)\n469:- `game.advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {})`: run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headles\n470:- `game.after(seconds, fn)`: run fn once after `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change. Returns { cancel() }\n471:- `game.allowJoin(o = {})` †: let players join, up to max (2..4): Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard (o.keys: false not); onJoin (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are String's and Array's)\n472:- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() takes it (it returns true when it did); else a scene in pla\n473:- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n474:- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests an\n475:- `game.enableGPU(o = {})` †: turn on WebGPU lighting: where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with its own ambient (game.lights.ambient) and the same lights. opts: { map, bands, ambient, fallback, ... }\n476:- `game.every(seconds, fn)`: run fn every `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change. Returns { cancel() }\n477:- `game.fail(where, e)`: report an error in game code without stopping the game: logged once per place and message, kept in game.errors and shown on the error panel (the engine reports its own callbacks' errors this way): game.fail('power ' + id, err)\n478:- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n479:- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n480:- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n481:- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n482:- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', 'iris', 'mosaic',\n483:- `game.hitFx(x, y, z, o = {})`: everything a good hit needs in one call: hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks along the hit direction, a sound, a damage number and a rumble on a gamepad. hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage, color, sound: 'hit', ru\n484:- `game.menu(name)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls'\n485:- `game.mouseGround()`: mouse position on the ground (world units), or null\n486:- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n487:- `game.note(text, seconds = 1.2)`, `game.shownNote`: a short notice at the top of the screen (zoom level, saved, ...): game.note('ZOOM 1.5x', 1). game.shownNote is the one showing, { text, end } (end in game.real seconds), or null: a game that draws it it\n488:- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n489:- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() does it from code\n491:- `game.removePlayer(p)` †: a player leaves (its input, or its number, 2 to 4); player 1 has the whole keyboard back when no one shares it\n492:- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n493:- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n494:- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n495:- `game.saves()` †: game.saves: the save slots (section 10k), made when first read\n496:- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits: a number (seco\n497:- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n498:- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles and lights are \n499:- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n501:- `game.splitScreen(n, o = {})` †: split the screen into n viewports (2 to 4; 0 or 1: one screen again), o: { layout: 'auto' (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one; a grid for 3 or 4) | 'columns' | 'rows' | 'grid', follow: [targets], z: 16, \n502:- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pausable, view, vi\n503:- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n504:- `game.start({ back })`: a scene's back() takes a phone's back gesture (game.back()) first: return true when it went back somewhere (a sub-menu closed)\n505:- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, on top of the game's; false or none: they can't; a functi\n506:- `game.start({ boot })`: { title, version, steps: [[label, fn]], messages, min, checks, accent } runs the boot first (section 10c): the steps one a frame behind the boot screen (all at once when headless or loop: false); boot: false hides a splash\n507:- `game.stop()`: stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it\n508:- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(target), keep (out\n511:- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom range, kept as their setting (settings.zoom, on top of \n512:- `game.zoomRange()` †: the zooms the player may take the camera to here, [min, max] (the scene's camera.zoom, or the game's; a function of the game gives one per screen shape or level), or null where they can't (camera.zoom false or none)\n640:- `new E.Attack(spec = {}, over) → slash`: new E.Attack(spec) or new E.Attack('slash', { reach: 9 }): a name from E.MOVES plus overrides\n666:- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Characters\", lists the options); update(dt, state) each step, d\n713:- `new E.Blob(o = {}) → blob`: new E.Blob({ R: 6.5, colors, ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face }): a squash-and-stretch rig for slimes and jellies; blob.update(dt, state) each step, blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view) in an r.actor\n733:- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], typ\n734:- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n735:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n736:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n737:- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0432b045-5008-4207-9914-efcb595d2dad","uuid":"49445cd0-7355-4157-b90d-2e44ce415c7a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:19:24.008Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"197:- `px.blend(g, a, mode, fn)`: draw fn() at alpha a (stepped, like PS1 hardware) with a blend mode: 'normal', 'add', 'multiply', 'screen' (E.style.trans = 'dither' dithers instead)\n198:- `px.col(g, c)`: set the fill color (px.* do it themselves)\n199:- `px.ddisc(g, cx, cy, r, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: soft disc that fades toward its edge (glows, smoke, dust): stepped alpha rings, or dithering in 'dither' style\n200:- `px.disc(g, cx, cy, r, c)`: a filled circle\n201:- `px.dot(g, x, y, c)`: one pixel\n202:- `px.ell(g, cx, cy, rx, ry, c)`: a filled ellipse\n203:- `px.glow(g, e = 1)`: GPU lighting: what follows glows (0..1). No effect in Canvas mode, so call it freely.\n204:- `px.line(g, x0, y0, x1, y1, c, w = 1)`: a line w pixels thick, no smoothing\n205:- `px.poly(g, pts, c)`: a filled polygon through [[x, y], ...]\n206:- `px.polyDither(g, pts, c, a, wx = 0, wy = 0)`: translucent polygon: a = 0..1 opacity (stepped alpha, or a dither pattern in 'dither' style). wx/wy = world pixel offset for the pattern\n207:- `px.rect(g, x, y, w, h, c)`: a filled rectangle, snapped to whole pixels\n208:- `px.reset(g)`: forget the color cache (after drawing on g with the canvas API directly)\n209:- `px.sprite(g, spr, x, y, flip = false)`: draw a sprite with its top-left corner at buffer pixel (x, y). flip mirrors it left-right; or o: { flip, rot (radians, turned about its center: E.rotSprite), steps, scale (nearest-neighbour) }\n223:- `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale = 1, zBoost = 1) → view`: new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost): an orthographic camera (degrees; scale = screen pixels per world unit; zBoost stretches heights)\n276:- `input.anyPressed()`: any action pressed this step (title screens: \"press any button\")\n277:- `input.bindButtons(root)` †: wire every element with data-act=\"<action>\" inside root to the action\n279:- `input.box()` †: the pointer's gestures, read each step in screen pixels (the HUD's space), from a mouse or one finger: box() -> { x0, y0, x1, y1, from, to, done } while a box is drawn (a mouse's left button dragged; on a touch screen a long press, then \n280:- `input.buffered(a, win = .15)`: was it pressed within the last `win` seconds and not yet consumed? (input buffering)\n281:- `input.clear()`: Release all devices and discard buffered input on focus loss or a modal transition.\n282:- `input.clicked()` †: where a click or tap on the canvas landed, in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space: r.W, r.H), during the step after it lifted (a press that drags away, like the touch stick's, is none); else null. Menus and dialogs answer to it; a \n283:- `input.codeLabel(code)`: a code as a person reads it: 'W' (or the keyboard's own letter there), 'Space', '↑', 'Left click', and a gamepad button by its symbol on the controller in use ('A', '✕', 'B'...; glyphs picks the family)\n284:- `input.consume(a)`: mark a buffered press of action a as used, so buffered(a) is false until the next press\n285:- `input.consumeAll()`: forget every pending press (after closing a menu or dialog, so the same press does not also attack)\n286:- `input.doubleTap()` †: [x, y] the step a second tap or click lands within .35 s and 8 pixels of the first (a double tap), else null\n287:- `input.down(a)`: is the action held right now?\n288:- `input.drag()` †: how far the touch stick's finger moved since the last step, in screen pixels [dx, dy] (the HUD's space), or null while no finger holds it: to steer one to one (a shooter's ship follows the thumb) instead of by the stick's direction\n289:- `input.feed(type, x, y, o = {})` †: a pointer event in screen pixels, as the browser's would give: feed('down' | 'move' | 'up', x, y, { touch: false, button: 0, id: 0 (which finger, for zones) }) (a test's or a bot's mouse and fingers: clicks, boxes, lon\n291:- `input.hold(a, on = true)`: hold an action down (on) or let it go (false), as its key would\n292:- `input.label(a, device = this.lastDevice)`: the key or button to show in a prompt for action a, on the device the player used last (or the one named: 'keyboard' | 'mouse' | 'gamepad' | 'touch'), with their bindings: input.label('jump') -> 'Space', 'A' or\n293:- `input.longPress()` †: [x, y] the step a press held still has been held .45 s (a long press), else null\n294:- `input.motion(digits, button, o = {})` †: a fighter's move in numpad notation, mirrored by facing: input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing, window }) is a quarter circle forward and punch (down, down-forward, forward, attack: a fireball); '62\n295:- `input.mouseScreen()`: mouse position in projected screen pixels, or null\n296:- `input.move()`: movement intent in screen directions: x right, y down, length <= 1\n297:- `input.padStyle()`: the controller family whose symbols label() shows: glyphs, or with glyphs 'auto' the one its name says\n298:- `input.pan()` †: [dx, dy] how far a drag moved this step: one finger on open ground (not a box), or a mouse's middle or right button; else null. A free camera pans by it (game.cam.free)\n299:- `input.pointer()` †: the mouse pointer in screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space), or null before it has moved over the canvas\n300:- `input.press(code)`: press a key or button code ('KeyJ', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0', 'Act:attack') as if the person did: scripted input, tests, bots\n301:- `input.pressed(a)`: true only during the first update step after the press (menus, jumps, one-shot actions)\n302:- `input.rebind(a, device, code)` †: bind action a to one code for a device ('keys' = keyboard and mouse, 'pad' = gamepad), in place of that device's codes: input.rebind('attack', 'keys', 'KeyF'). Returns null when done, the action that already uses the co\n303:- `input.rebindable(a, map = this.preset)` †: can the player rebind action a? Every action but the menu's (start, pause, confirm, cancel: they are how a player gets back to the menu), and those the game hides with new E.Game({ menu: { controls: { a: false \n304:- `input.release(code)`: let go of a code pressed with press()\n305:- `input.released(a)`: true only during the first update step after the action was let go\n306:- `input.repeat(a, delay = .3, rate = .08)`: pressed, then repeats while held (after `delay` s, every `rate` s): menu cursors, puzzle pieces\n307:- `input.resetBindings()` †: forget the player's bindings: every action back to its preset's codes\n308:- `input.rumble(strength = .5, seconds = .15)` †: shake the gamepad: strength 0..1 for seconds (scaled by rumbleScale; nothing where the controller can't). Returns true when it rumbled\n309:- `input.screenPoint(cx, cy)` †: browser client coords -> screen pixels [x, y] (the HUD's space, with no camera), or null without a canvas\n310:- `input.sequence(steps, o = {})` †: true on the step a sequence of inputs ends, each one after the last within window seconds (.5) of the first: input.sequence(['down', 'down+right', 'right', 'attack']). A step names actions joined by +: its directions ar\n311:- `input.stopRumble()` †: stop a rumble now (pause, focus lost, unplugged)\n312:- `input.suppress(a)` †: Suppress a held action until the next tick (a HUD widget owns the pointer this step).\n313:- `input.suppressCode(code)` †: A widget owns this button; other devices bound to the same action still work.\n314:- `input.tap(a)`: press an action for one update step, as a quick tap of its key (scripted input, tests, an AI): pressed() and down() are true in the next step, released() in the one after\n315:- `input.tick(dt)`: advance input by one step: read the gamepad and turn presses since the last step into this step's pressed() and released() (the engine calls it every step)\n316:- `input.touchArea()` †: where the touch buttons cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (the same space as r.W and r.H), or null while none show: keep a prompt or a box clear of it (a bottom E.Dialog does by itself)\n317:- `input.touchButtons(actions, o = {})` †: On-screen buttons for phones and tablets (shown on touch screens; the player's settings can show them always or hide them, put them on the left, and size them): input.touchButtons(['jump', 'attack'], { labels: { j\n318:- `input.touchSet(a, label = null, show = true)` †: one touch button as the moment changes: its label (TALK beside someone, NEXT in a dialog; null: its own) and whether it shows (false: nothing for it to do here). Cheap to call every step: input.touchSet('\n319:- `input.unbind(a, device)` †: take away action a's codes for one device ('keys' or 'pad'); the arrow keys and d-pad stay on the moves\n320:- `input.use(map)`: switch key layout at runtime: input.use(E.Input.PLATFORMER) or input.use('SHMUP'). The player's own bindings (bindings, set by rebind() and the Controls screen) are laid over it. Every preset (E.Input.DEFAULT, PLATFORMER, THIRDPERSON, S\n321:- `input.zoom()` †: the camera zoom the player asked for since the last step, as a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther; 1: none): the wheel, - and = and a pinch, where the scene lets them zoom. The game applies it (game.zoomBy)\n322:- `input.zoomPoint()` †: [x, y] where the last zoom was asked for: the mouse pointer for the wheel, between the fingers for a pinch; null when the keys zoomed (about the screen's middle)\n407:- `r.actor(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: Queue a character. drawFn(g, ox, oy) must draw with (ox, oy) as the projected position of world point (x, y, z). The engine adds outline, rim light, hit flash, afterimages and x-ray silhouettes. opts: {outline=true, ri\n408:- `r.actor({ xray })`: true shows the actor's hidden part (a translucent tint with a 1-px outline, r.xrayColor and r.xrayOutline; a checker in E.style.trans 'dither') wherever a wall, a prop or shape at least chest high, or anything queued with hides is dr\n409:- `r.actor({ hides })`: true: this actor counts as an x-ray occluder even when no x-ray actor asked for actors\n410:- `r.actor({ ghost })`: ghost.owner (any {x, y, z}, usually the actor itself) keeps its afterimages sorted behind the live body\n411:- `r.actor({ every, key })`: every: n, key (any object, the character's own): its finished image is drawn again for n - 1 frames before drawFn runs again, at the place it is now (a background character animating at a lower rate, as classic games' townsfolk\n412:- `r.actor({ wrap })`: [w, h], as r.sprite's: a world that wraps round shows the actor across each edge it crosses\n413:- `r.actorNow(x, y, z, drawFn, o = {})`: draw an actor at once, over what is drawn so far, not sorted by depth (a pass of the game's own: a body falling into a void painted over the level), with r.actor's options (outline, flash, alpha, ...)\n415:- `r.box(g, x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1, top, side)`: extruded box prop (crate, pedestal, table). Call inside a queue fn with its g.\n418:- `r.decal(fn, o)`: draw on the floor before the queue. opts.emissive (0..1) makes it glow under GPU lighting\n419:- `r.glowDisc(g, cx, cy, rad, color, a)`: glow halo from a cache: additive light rings (alpha style) or a dithered disc (dither style). a = 0..1 strength\n420:- `r.groundArc(x, y, r0, r1, a0, a1, color, alpha = 1, z = 0, g = this.tgt)`: a band of a ring on the ground from radius r0 to r1 and angle a0 to a1 (radians): melee telegraphs, sight cones\n421:- `r.groundDisc(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a dithered disc lying on the ground (radius in world units, z its height): shadows, auras, area attacks\n422:- `r.groundPts(x, y, rad, n = 18, z = 0)`: n screen points round a circle of radius rad on the ground at (x, y), height z: the outline of a ring or a fan\n423:- `r.groundRing(x, y, rad, color, alpha = 1, z = 0)`: a ring lying on the ground (radius in world units)\n424:- `r.marker(x, y, z = 0, o = {})`: an objective marker over a world point: a bobbing diamond (and its label) while it is on screen, an arrow at the screen's edge pointing to it while it is off (offscreen: false hides it then). o: { label, color: '#ffd040',\n425:- `r.overlay(fn)`: draw fn(g) on top of everything, in screen pixels (HUD, dialog, menus)\n426:- `r.pick(sx, sy)` †: what was drawn under a screen point (the HUD's pixels: input.clicked(), input.pointer()) in the last frame, front to back: the pick values of the actors, sprites, props and queued draws given one (r.actor(x, y, z, fn, { pick: unit }))\n427:- `r.prop(name, x, y, z, o = {})` †: set dressing from E.prop: r.prop('torch', x, y, z, { size, color, flip, light: true, halo: true, bias, hides, footprint }) feet on the world point; flames animate and glow. light: true also adds a warm light to game.lig\n428:- `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, o)`: queue a depth-sorted draw. fn(g) draws into g. opts: {bias, occluder, hides, xray, emissive, solid (hides a glow behind it), footprint} occluder: a wall (x-ray silhouettes show through it, it sorts as a block). hides: only lets\n429:- `r.shadow(x, y, rad, alpha = .5, color = '#0c0818', z = 0)`: soft contact shadow under a character: a cached dithered ellipse, correct for the current view. z = height of the surface it falls on (0 = floor; the top of a block or platform otherwise)\n430:- `r.shape(id, x, y, z = 0, o = {})` †: Queue a shape standing at (x, y, z), feet on the point, depth sorted like an actor: r.shape(id | entry, x, y, z, o). o: { facing (radians, where its front points; default PI/2, south, as written), scale, mats: { slot\n431:- `r.sightCone(obs, o = {})` †: an observer's view on the ground: a dithered fan fov wide (radians) about its facing, out to range, cut short where the map hides a body's chest (map.ray, the height rules), draped over the ground below. o: { range: 120, fov\n432:- `r.sky(colors, o = {})`: background of dithered color bands, top to bottom: r.sky(['#0b1030', '#3b2d6b', '#e0806a']). Call first in draw.\n433:- `r.sprite(x, y, z, spr, o = {})`: Queue a pixel sprite (from E.sprite) standing at world point (x, y, z). Depth sorted like an actor. opts: { flip, anchor: 'bottom' (feet on the point, default; E.shapeSprite's: its own feet) | 'center' | 'top', glow: 0..\n434:- `r.starfield(o = {})`: scrolling star dots for space and night skies. opts: { count: 70, speed: 30 px/s, dir: 'down' | 'up' | 'left' | 'right', colors, layers: 3, seed }\n435:- `r.text(str, x, y, color = '#ffffff', o)`: HUD text in screen pixels, drawn on top and never lit: r.text('SCORE 120', 4, 4, '#fff', { align, scale, outline })\n436:- `r.textAt(x, y, z, str, color = '#ffffff', o = {})`: text floating at a world point (names, prompts): r.textAt(x, y, z, 'OPEN', '#ffe08a')\n439:- `r.visible(x, y, z = 0, mx = 60, up = 30, down = 90)`: is a world point near the visible area? margins in pixels (characters extend upward from their feet)\n440:- `r.w(x, y, z = 0)`: world -> buffer pixel coords\n453:- `new E.Game(o = {}) → game`: new E.Game({ canvas, view, views, res, expand, input, bg, step: 1 / 120, loop: true, headless: false, menu, fullscreen: 'tap', corner: true, backGesture: true, autoPause: true, id, pace, camera }). The standard features (sect\n454:- `new E.Game({ res })`: a fixed resolution ('ps1' or [w, h]), whole pixels\n455:- `new E.Game({ expand })`: true makes res the least the screen shows and grows the picture to fill the window, any shape, no bars (expand: { maxW, maxH } stops it growing there)\n456:- `new E.Game({ bg })`: the color behind everything, or null: the canvas is clear, and the game draws its HUD and menus over a picture under it (a 3D world, engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js)\n457:- `new E.Game({ headless })`: no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input (tests, tools, a simulation in Node, where it is the default): scenes update and draw is skipped; drive it with advance() and input.press / release / tap / hold\n458:- `new E.Game({ loop })`: false makes a game in a page that runs only by advance()\n459:- `new E.Game({ menu })`: the pause, settings and controls screens ({ items, settings, controls, quit } adjusts them, false leaves them out)\n460:- `new E.Game({ fullscreen })`: 'tap' (a phone goes full screen at its first tap and when a game starts, unless the player turned it off), 'button' (only when asked) or false\n461:- `new E.Game({ corner, backGesture })`: corner: the menu and full-screen buttons (top right); backGesture: a phone's back steps back in the game first (false: it leaves the page)\n462:- `new E.Game({ autoPause })`: a pausable scene pauses when the page is hidden or loses focus\n463:- `new E.Game({ id })`: the name the player's settings are saved under (the page's path)\n464:- `new E.Game({ pace })`: false draws every refresh of a fast display even when the frames can't keep up (by default it draws every other one, evenly), unless the player picks Frame rate 60\n465:- `new E.Game({ camera })`: the camera of every scene without its own (a scene's camera option: the player's zoom, start)\n468:- `game.addPlayer(o = {})` †: a player in now: { pad: index } (a gamepad) or { keys: 'right' } (the keyboard's right half; player 1 takes the left). Returns its input (null when four play already)\n469:- `game.advance(seconds = this.o.step, o = {})`: run the game for `seconds` of real time in exact fixed steps, at once, without requestAnimationFrame: tests, tools and headless runs (advance(1) is 120 updates). o.draw: then draw one frame (not in a headles\n470:- `game.after(seconds, fn)`: run fn once after `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change. Returns { cancel() }\n471:- `game.allowJoin(o = {})` †: let players join, up to max (2..4): Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard (o.keys: false not); onJoin (input, number) as each does. allowJoin(false) closes it (the names split and join are String's and Array's)\n472:- `game.back()`: a step back, as a phone's back gesture asks for (the engine calls it for one; a back button of the game's own can): the open menu screen goes back one; else the scene's own back() takes it (it returns true when it did); else a scene in pla\n473:- `game.cornerArea()` †: where the corner's buttons (menu and full screen, top right; the touch START) cover the screen, in screen pixels { x0, y0, x1, y1 } (r.W and r.H's space), or null while none show: keep a HUD clear of it\n474:- `game.dispose()`: end the game for good: stop the loop and remove everything the engine added to the page (its listeners on the window, the document, the canvas and the buttons, the touch controls, the GPU lighting's canvas) and close the sound. Tests an\n475:- `game.enableGPU(o = {})` †: turn on WebGPU lighting: where it can't draw (no WebGPU, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with its own ambient (game.lights.ambient) and the same lights. opts: { map, bands, ambient, fallback, ... }\n476:- `game.every(seconds, fn)`: run fn every `seconds` of game time. Cleared on scene change. Returns { cancel() }\n477:- `game.fail(where, e)`: report an error in game code without stopping the game: logged once per place and message, kept in game.errors and shown on the error panel (the engine reports its own callbacks' errors this way): game.fail('power ' + id, err)\n478:- `game.flash(color = '#ffffff', seconds = .12, strength = 1)`: flash the whole screen with a color for `seconds` (dithered fade out): hits, explosions, lightning. Shorter when reduceMotion, dimmer by the player's settings.flashes\n479:- `game.focus(x, y, z = 0)`: camera follows this world point. Side-scrollers: game.focus(x, 0, z)\n480:- `game.follow(target, o = {})`: keep the camera on an object automatically (instead of calling focus every update). opts: { z: extra height to look at (default 16), lead: look ahead in the facing / moving direction }. follow(null) stops.\n481:- `game.freeze(s)`: hit-stop: the game stands still for s seconds (.04-.08 for a hit); input still ticks\n482:- `game.go(name, data, o = {})`: switch scene: game.go('play', { level: 2 }). It fades through black (dithered) unless opts.fade is 0 (a cut) or another number of seconds; opts.fade can name a transition instead: 'black', 'white', 'wipe', 'iris', 'mosaic',\n483:- `game.hitFx(x, y, z, o = {})`: everything a good hit needs in one call: hit-stop, shake, an impact star, sparks along the hit direction, a sound, a damage number and a rumble on a gamepad. hitFx(x, y, z, { power: 1, angle, damage, color, sound: 'hit', ru\n484:- `game.menu(name)`: open the menu (the corner's menu button and the touch START do it): a pausable scene (or a game started without scenes) pauses on its pause screen; any other scene, a title, waits under the settings. menu('settings') or menu('controls'\n485:- `game.mouseGround()`: mouse position on the ground (world units), or null\n486:- `game.nextView(dir = 1)`: cycle through game.views (or every view): bind it to a key for players who like to switch\n487:- `game.note(text, seconds = 1.2)`, `game.shownNote`: a short notice at the top of the screen (zoom level, saved, ...): game.note('ZOOM 1.5x', 1). game.shownNote is the one showing, { text, end } (end in game.real seconds), or null: a game that draws it it\n488:- `game.onView(f)` †: call f(view) whenever the view changes (re-bake caches, move HUD)\n489:- `game.pause(reason = 'player')`: pause the game, saying why ('player' | 'focus' | 'controller' | 'menu': pauseReason); returns true if it paused. The player resumes (Escape, Start, Resume); resume() does it from code\n491:- `game.removePlayer(p)` †: a player leaves (its input, or its number, 2 to 4); player 1 has the whole keyboard back when no one shares it\n492:- `game.resetCamera()` †: undo setZoom and rotateView: the scene's own zoom again (camera.start, or camera.tall on a screen held upright; 1 without), no turn. The player's zoom stays (settings.zoom)\n493:- `game.resume()`: take the game out of pause (and close the pause screen)\n494:- `game.rotateView(deg)` †: turn the camera around the vertical axis in ground views: game.rotateView(45) (degrees). Side views never turn\n495:- `game.saves()` †: game.saves: the save slots (section 10k), made when first read\n496:- `game.script(fn, o)` †: run a coroutine on game time, in the update step: game.script(function* (game) { yield .5; yield E.until(() => door.open); yield game.tween(cam, { y: 0 }, 1); yield E.all([a, b]); ... }, { keep }). Each yield waits: a number (seco\n497:- `game.setView(v)`: switch the view: an id from E.VIEWS ('iso', 'side', ...) or a View; keeps zoom and turn\n498:- `game.setZoom(z)` †: camera zoom on top of the view: game.setZoom(1.5) (.5 .. 3), the game's own; where the scene lets the player zoom (camera.zoom), theirs multiplies it (game.zoom is the two together). Characters, maps, props, particles and lights are \n499:- `game.shake(a)`: shake the screen by a (pixels, decaying; at most 10 in all); none when reduceMotion, scaled by the player's settings.shake\n501:- `game.splitScreen(n, o = {})` †: split the screen into n viewports (2 to 4; 0 or 1: one screen again), o: { layout: 'auto' (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one; a grid for 3 or 4) | 'columns' | 'rows' | 'grid', follow: [targets], z: 16, \n502:- `game.start(hooks)`: Start the loop. Either one scene: game.start({ update(dt), draw(r) }) or several: game.start({ scenes: { title: {...}, play: {...} }, scene: 'title' }) A scene is { enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), back(), pausable, view, vi\n503:- `game.start({ view, views, input, res, expand, touch })`: a scene's, applied when it starts: input ('PLATFORMER'), res ('snes'), expand (true), touch (['jump', 'attack']); res and expand stay until another scene sets them\n504:- `game.start({ back })`: a scene's back() takes a phone's back gesture (game.back()) first: return true when it went back somewhere (a sub-menu closed)\n505:- `game.start({ camera })`: a scene's { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5, wheel: true }: the player zooms the camera within zoom (the wheel, - and =, a pinch, Settings, Camera zoom: theirs, kept, on top of the game's; false or none: they can't; a functi\n506:- `game.start({ boot })`: { title, version, steps: [[label, fn]], messages, min, checks, accent } runs the boot first (section 10c): the steps one a frame behind the boot screen (all at once when headless or loop: false); boot: false hides a splash\n507:- `game.stop()`: stop the loop; the game keeps its state, and start() resumes it\n508:- `game.tween(target, to, seconds, o)` †: ease target's numbers to new values over seconds of game time: game.tween(door, { y: 40 }, .5, { ease: 'outBack', delay, repeat (more plays; -1 forever), yoyo (every other play runs back), onDone(target), keep (out\n511:- `game.zoomBy(k)` †: the player zooms the camera by a factor (1.1: 10% closer, .9: farther), as the wheel, - and = and a pinch do (a zoom button of the game's own can): within the scene's camera.zoom range, kept as their setting (settings.zoom, on top of \n512:- `game.zoomRange()` †: the zooms the player may take the camera to here, [min, max] (the scene's camera.zoom, or the game's; a function of the game gives one per screen shape or level), or null where they can't (camera.zoom false or none)\n640:- `new E.Attack(spec = {}, over) → slash`: new E.Attack(spec) or new E.Attack('slash', { reach: 9 }): a name from E.MOVES plus overrides\n666:- `new E.Humanoid(o = {}) → rig`: new E.Humanoid({ size: 1.3, build, weapon, outfit, hair, hat, sleeves, armor, hood, cape, colors, ... }): a character rig posed from math every step (API.md, \"Characters\", lists the options); update(dt, state) each step, d\n713:- `new E.Blob(o = {}) → blob`: new E.Blob({ R: 6.5, colors, ears, horns, wings, feet, tail, mouth, face }): a squash-and-stretch rig for slimes and jellies; blob.update(dt, state) each step, blob.draw(g, ox, oy, view) in an r.actor\n733:- `new E.TileMap(o = {}) → map`: Walls standing on a floor (top-down, isometric, brawler). Either { rows: [...ASCII...], legend: { '#': 1, '@': 'hero' }, types, floorTex } (recommended) or { w, h, cells: [ids...], floor: [tags...], blocked: [0 | 1...], typ\n734:- `new E.TileMap({ types })`: types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH, face, roof }\n735:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].face })`: the wall's material (E.MATERIALS): 'brick' (default) | 'stone' | 'rock' | 'plank' | 'timber' | 'plaster' | 'hedge' | 'none' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n736:- `new E.TileMap({ types[id].roof })`: the top's material: 'speckle' (default) | 'plain' | 'tiles' (roof shingles) | 'slab' (paving) | 'leaves' | 'grass' | any material id or '#hex pattern'\n737:- `new E.TileMap({ floorTex })`: floorTex(x, y, floorTag, view) -> [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, glow]","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012tTcix8jkGLAHsLAqbB6Nj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
